WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.010
- Hi, good evening, everyone.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:09.861
I'm Priya Natarajan,

00:00:09.861 --> 00:00:11.887
the current director
of the Franke Program.

00:00:11.887 --> 00:00:13.690
I'm an astrophysicist,
and I'm a faculty member

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:16.560
in the departments of physics
and astronomy at Yale.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.780
I am deeply invested in
interdisciplinary scholarship

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:21.360
as you've probably guessed.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.400
And thanks to my academic training

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.920
and a sort of an intellectual interest

00:00:28.773 --> 00:00:30.170
in the history and philosophy of science.

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:32.680
I'm very fortunate to be
leading the Franke Program

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:36.190
which is the flagship
cross-disciplinary program at Yale.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:38.030
First, I would like to start

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:39.690
by recognizing the generosity

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.165
and support of our benefactors,

00:00:42.165 --> 00:00:45.029
Mr. and Mrs. Richard and Barbara Franke

00:00:45.029 --> 00:00:47.529
for all they do for us and for Yale.

00:00:47.529 --> 00:00:50.640
I'm delighted that they're
going to be joining us today.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.110
First, I would also like to remind you too

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:55.483
that we are recording this event,

00:00:55.483 --> 00:00:58.230
and that all participants must
therefore mute their videos.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:02.480
If you wish you can submit
your questions after the talk

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.550
through the chat feature.

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:05.890
Our speaker this evening

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:08.688
is one of our own brilliant scholars,

00:01:08.688 --> 00:01:10.888
Professor Alan Mikhail,
Professor of History

00:01:11.811 --> 00:01:14.250
and the current chair of the
Department of History at Yale.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.300
Alan is a preeminent historian

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.480
of the early modern Muslim world,

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.123
the Ottoman empire and Egypt.

00:01:21.123 --> 00:01:23.220
His research and teaching focuses mostly

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.980
on the history of
empires and environments,

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:29.440
both matters that are incredibly
important to understand

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.700
beyond the confines of academia

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:34.410
as we all venture to make sense

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.140
of today's complex globally interconnected

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.150
and yet fractured world,

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:41.720
rife with inequities of
opportunity, health and wealth

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:44.690
and the looming climate catastrophe

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:48.170
brought by uncurtailed human
activities over history

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:51.060
and their damaging impact
on the environment.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.390
He received his PhD

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:54.330
from the University of
California, Berkeley

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:58.631
where his dissertation
won the Malcolm Kerr Award

00:01:58.631 --> 00:01:59.680
of the Middle East Studies Association

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.930
and Berkeley's own James Kepner award.

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:05.670
He was a member of the Andrew
Mann Fellowship of Scholars

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.390
in the humanities and was
at Stanford University

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:10.513
before coming to Yale in 2010.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.910
He's the author of many acclaimed books,

00:02:15.477 --> 00:02:18.360
the first of which "The
Animal in Ottoman Egypt"

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.097
came out in 2014,

00:02:20.097 --> 00:02:22.137
"Nature and Empire in Ottoman Egypt:

00:02:22.137 --> 00:02:25.490
"an Environmental History"
that came out in 2011.

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:26.810
He's also the editor

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:29.865
of "Water on Sand: Environmental Histories

00:02:29.865 --> 00:02:31.460
"of the Middle East and North Africa."

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:34.590
So his articles, research
articles, have appeared

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:39.050
in all the eminent journals
across disciplines.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:41.560
And he's the recipient of
many awards and honors.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.450
In the interest of time,
I will just mention a few:

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:47.950
the Roger Owen Award of the
Middle East Studies Association,

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:49.600
the Alice Hamilton prize

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.940
of the American Society
for Environmental History,

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:56.160
the Wayne Rasmussen Prize

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.320
of the Agricultural History Society,

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.810
and the Yale's Gustav Ranis

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:05.690
and Samuel and Ronnie Heyman prizes.

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:08.260
He teaches undergraduate
and graduate courses

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:11.790
in the history of the
early modern Muslim world

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.850
and the Mediterranean, the Ottoman empire

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:15.590
and environmental history.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:19.160
So before I hand over to him,
I just wanted to remind you

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.870
of a few upcoming Frankie Program events.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:25.020
We have the author and
science communicator,

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:29.067
Ainissa Ramirez, former
Yale faculty member

00:03:29.067 --> 00:03:31.630
giving a talk on April 20th at 4:00 p.m.

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:34.380
And we have the Inference
Project Colloquium

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:38.720
by the philosopher Lorenzo
Magnani on April 22nd.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:41.163
And then we have Lyanda Lynn Haupt,

00:03:42.329 --> 00:03:43.687
the author of "Mozart's Starling"

00:03:43.687 --> 00:03:45.310
who will be speaking to us on April 27th.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:48.440
So we have a full roster for April,

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.023
and we also have,

00:03:50.903 --> 00:03:53.790
are planning to show
the movie "Coded Bias,"

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:57.750
and have a discussion with
the filmmaker on April 21st.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:00.860
And so I would also like to remind you all

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:03.410
to look at our two other ongoing projects,

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:06.250
the Learning by Doing
Project on our website

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:07.210
and the inference,

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:09.623
Understanding the Nature
of Inference project.

00:04:10.571 --> 00:04:12.790
So now to today's event today.

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:16.712
Today Alan will be talking
to us about his new book,

00:04:16.712 --> 00:04:20.163
"God's Shadow: Sultan
Selim, His Ottoman Empire,

00:04:20.163 --> 00:04:21.500
"and the Making of the Modern World."

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:25.207
And the title of his talk
is "Columbus and Islam."

00:04:26.453 --> 00:04:28.930
And this is an encounter
that I'm extremely excited

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:30.930
and curious to hear about.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:33.883
Thank you so much, Alan, for
agreeing to speak to us today.

00:04:35.711 --> 00:04:39.910
- Thank you, Priya,
very much for having me.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:42.330
I want to begin by extending my thanks

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:47.040
to you for the invitation,
to Ty and to Guy

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.470
for helping to organize today's event.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:52.570
Thanks to everybody for tuning in

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:55.393
on this afternoon in March.

00:04:56.318 --> 00:04:59.240
I wish we could do it in person,
but one day, one day soon.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:04.240
I'm gonna go ahead and
begin by sharing my screen.

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:07.140
And as I do that, of course,

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:10.780
allow me also to thank the Frankie family

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:14.100
for this lecture series

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:17.363
and for all of their support
of what we do at Yale.

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:22.920
So as Priya said, this talk
is coming out of a book

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:27.300
that I just published that's
really focused on the life

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:32.280
of a particular Ottoman
Sultan, Selim the First

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:34.510
the ninth Sultan of the Ottoman Empire

00:05:35.676 --> 00:05:37.300
who lived from 1470 to 1520.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:40.160
I'm really not gonna be
talking much about him today.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.410
I'm gonna be focusing
in on a part of the book

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:48.070
that addresses part of the
role of the Ottoman Empire

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:51.480
and of other Muslim polities
around the Mediterranean

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:54.059
in the shaping of Columbus.

00:05:54.059 --> 00:05:55.436
This is something that I go to,

00:05:55.436 --> 00:05:59.170
go in depth into in the book.

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:02.020
So if you, if you're
curious about how this story

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:06.020
fits into a larger story
about the Ottoman Empire

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:07.850
and the early modern world,

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:10.293
I'd encourage you to
take a look at the book.

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:16.120
So two narratives dominate
the story of the conjoining

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:20.400
of the continents of the
old world and the Americas,

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.113
peopling and de-peopling,

00:06:23.464 --> 00:06:24.910
that is conquest and genocide,

00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:27.330
nation building and colonization,

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:29.290
the migration of Europeans

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:32.830
and the forced movement of
enslaved Africans on the one hand

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:34.860
and the displacement and death

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:37.643
of millions of indigenous
Americans on the other.

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:41.150
These are obviously very
familiar narratives,

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:44.043
narratives that work quite well.

00:06:44.890 --> 00:06:47.390
Much of the arc of the
story that they tell

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:50.306
is useful for us

00:06:50.306 --> 00:06:53.053
in thinking about the history
of the Atlantic world.

00:06:53.999 --> 00:06:56.270
And much of it does a
lot of very good work

00:06:56.270 --> 00:07:00.535
for our understanding of how we came to be

00:07:00.535 --> 00:07:01.603
in this historical moment.

00:07:02.539 --> 00:07:03.990
As with any historical framework though,

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:08.519
these grand narratives also
miss some things as well.

00:07:08.519 --> 00:07:09.536
So in my talk today

00:07:09.536 --> 00:07:12.539
I wanna argue for another grand narrative,

00:07:12.539 --> 00:07:15.670
one that I think needs to be
a part of the violent story

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:19.510
of the joining of the
four Atlantic continents.

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:21.940
And that is the role of the confrontation

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:23.880
between Islam and Christendom

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:26.030
in the shaping of this history.

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:29.560
It's an essential part of the
story, I'll try to show today.

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:32.260
It says an essential part of the story

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:35.470
of the colonization of the Americas,

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:40.300
the extension of Europe and
Africa across the ocean.

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:44.660
And it's essential that it be
a part of our understanding

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:45.963
of this history.

00:07:47.245 --> 00:07:50.868
Conflict and cooperation
between Islam and Christianity

00:07:50.868 --> 00:07:52.680
shaped the history of the Mediterranean,

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:56.190
perhaps more than any other single force.

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:59.250
And this was as true before 1492,

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:02.680
as it was after, some
of which is gestured to

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:04.383
in the quote that's on the screen.

00:08:06.332 --> 00:08:08.550
The Europeans who went to the Americas

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:11.350
were in many ways formed by
this geopolitical encounter.

00:08:12.221 --> 00:08:14.943
And as I'll discuss, it
remained a salient feature

00:08:14.943 --> 00:08:18.360
of their world even on the
other side of the ocean.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:20.880
Indeed one could tell a continuous story

00:08:21.724 --> 00:08:23.670
of the role of Islam in
the shaping of the history

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:27.173
of the Americas from 1492 to the present.

00:08:28.661 --> 00:08:33.240
This is a story that involves
slavery, piracy, trade,

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:36.500
the captivity of Europeans
in the Muslim world,

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:41.500
immigration, warfare, cultural
exchange and assimilation.

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:46.080
I won't be able to tell
that full story here today.

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:50.730
Rather I'm focusing obviously
on the very first moment

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:54.670
of this history, the one that
has rightly been freighted

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:56.850
with so much explanatory weight,

00:08:56.850 --> 00:09:01.850
in the narratives I mentioned
above, Columbus in 1492.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:05.870
I will ask how Islam shaped
Columbus and his voyages

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:09.000
and what its role was
in the very early years

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.733
of the European colonization
of the Caribbean.

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:14.930
This will be the bulk of my talk today.

00:09:14.930 --> 00:09:17.480
And at the end, I'll offer thoughts

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:21.666
about some of the
implications of the story

00:09:21.666 --> 00:09:23.766
that I'm telling about Columbus and Islam.

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:30.380
So let me begin the story
then where Columbus began,

00:09:30.606 --> 00:09:32.503
and that is in the city of Genoa.

00:09:34.222 --> 00:09:35.522
He was born there in 1451.

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:39.000
And even in the small neighborhood

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:41.810
he shared with his
parents and four siblings,

00:09:41.810 --> 00:09:44.410
Islam was a presence.

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:48.020
Columbus spent most of the
first two decades of his life

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:49.070
in that neighborhood.

00:09:50.149 --> 00:09:52.020
He would have seen or
heard about the many fleets

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:56.825
leaving for Jerusalem on
one of the numerous crusades

00:09:56.825 --> 00:09:58.300
that originated in Genoa.

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:01.450
Columbus's city was a magnet for crusaders

00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:03.300
owing to the strong presence

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:07.050
of the Knights of St. John
who offered free housing

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:09.853
and medical care to crusaders.

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:14.070
Columbus and his family
likely knew some crusaders,

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:18.120
and he no doubt grew up
hearing of their tales

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:20.853
of distant lands and infidel enemies.

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:25.620
It's important that Columbus
was born just two years before

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:28.400
the Ottoman Sultan Mehmed the Conqueror

00:10:29.383 --> 00:10:30.983
took the city of Constantinople.

00:10:33.228 --> 00:10:36.003
This the Bellini portrait of Mehmed.

00:10:37.205 --> 00:10:39.027
More than any other event,

00:10:39.027 --> 00:10:41.550
this conquest shaped the
contours of Columbus's,

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:44.833
and indeed his entire
generation's geopolitical life.

00:10:46.290 --> 00:10:49.910
Constant calls for European
crusades against the Ottomans

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:52.540
didn't lead to very
much in the second half

00:10:52.540 --> 00:10:54.360
of the 15th century.

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:58.120
Columbus's age was mostly
one of European stasis

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:00.453
in the face of Muslim ascendancy.

00:11:02.110 --> 00:11:05.210
In Genoa's San Lorenzo Cathedral,

00:11:05.210 --> 00:11:07.850
Columbus went to mass every week.

00:11:07.850 --> 00:11:09.880
This church held important treasures,

00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:13.183
supposedly captured
during earlier crusades.

00:11:14.403 --> 00:11:17.138
An Emerald green bowl said to be the dish

00:11:17.138 --> 00:11:19.600
Jesus washed his hands in
before the last supper,

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:21.310
and a silver reliquary

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:24.870
purported to contain the
ashes of John the Baptist.

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:28.330
Such trophies of conquest
were sprinkled all over Europe

00:11:28.330 --> 00:11:31.610
from the smallest towns
to the largest cathedrals.

00:11:31.610 --> 00:11:34.790
They brought the conflict
between Islam and Christianity

00:11:34.790 --> 00:11:38.240
into nearly every European
neighborhood church,

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:40.970
amulets of the looming threat of Islam

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:45.173
that had supposedly been
captured during earlier crusades.

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:49.000
In between Sundays at San Lorenzo,

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:51.670
Columbus would have learned
about other Europeans

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:54.043
who had traveled to the Muslim world.

00:11:54.910 --> 00:11:58.180
Even though it was a
vital Crusader entrepôt,

00:11:58.180 --> 00:12:01.893
Genoa was above all a mercantile
port not a military one.

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:05.950
Over the centuries the
Genoese merchant families

00:12:05.950 --> 00:12:09.550
who ran the city developed
many distant connections.

00:12:09.550 --> 00:12:14.283
Colonies of Genoese
merchants prospered in cities

00:12:14.283 --> 00:12:19.040
like Beirut, Alexandria,
Tunis, Oran, Algiers,

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:24.040
in Europe in places like Naples,
Paris, London, and so on.

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:27.740
Sometimes these Genoese trading families

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:32.370
even exercised something
akin to political control

00:12:32.370 --> 00:12:34.610
in some of these cities,

00:12:34.610 --> 00:12:38.493
especially around the Black Sea Coast.

00:12:39.490 --> 00:12:43.630
Genoa maintained very old
connections to ports and towns,

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:46.170
ports and towns that would
steadily be incorporated

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:51.170
into the Ottoman Empire in the
14th and the 15th centuries.

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:54.430
In Columbus's youth,
Genoa's merchant community

00:12:54.430 --> 00:12:57.570
was growing to be quite
anxious about Ottoman advances

00:12:57.570 --> 00:13:00.550
in the Black Sea and the Mediterranean,

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:04.225
and already mourning some of their losses

00:13:04.225 --> 00:13:05.225
around the two seas.

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:07.670
In addition--

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:08.663
- [Priya] Alan?

00:13:08.663 --> 00:13:09.496
- Yes.

00:13:09.496 --> 00:13:10.329
- I had a very quick question.

00:13:10.329 --> 00:13:13.890
So was Genoa actually a very
wealthy city at this point?

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:18.010
Or, I mean, so what was
the socioeconomic frame

00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:19.510
at this time?

00:13:19.510 --> 00:13:23.410
- Sure, Genoa is indeed
quite a wealthy city,

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:26.770
largely based on its trading
connections with the East.

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:27.603
Absolutely.

00:13:30.410 --> 00:13:33.290
And you get a sense from
this map of of the expanse

00:13:33.290 --> 00:13:34.740
of their trading connections.

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:39.340
So in addition to breathing
in the mercantile air

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:43.210
all around him in Genoa, Columbus
learned important lessons

00:13:43.210 --> 00:13:48.210
about the East by reading
the works of various writers,

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:52.040
and in particular, the
work of one other Italian

00:13:52.040 --> 00:13:57.040
who had spent time in some
of Genoa's Black Sea cities,

00:13:57.424 --> 00:13:58.573
and that's Marco Polo.

00:14:00.427 --> 00:14:02.580
Reading the famous tales of Marco polo,

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:07.300
and again, other accounts
of travels to distant lands

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:10.533
helped to form Columbus's
mental canvas as a boy.

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:13.590
One of the most important
things Columbus learned

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:16.930
from Marco Polo was the existence

00:14:16.930 --> 00:14:20.313
of a figure known as the
Grand Khan of the East.

00:14:21.290 --> 00:14:24.650
Marco supposedly arrived
in the opulent court

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:29.650
of this Grand Khan, far off
in some vague place in Asia,

00:14:31.205 --> 00:14:35.430
past Constantinople, Jerusalem,
Baghdad, and Afghanistan,

00:14:35.430 --> 00:14:36.330
where he writes

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:39.593
that he discovered a
jewel-encrusted paradise

00:14:39.593 --> 00:14:41.143
of luxury, learning, and power.

00:14:42.310 --> 00:14:43.890
What most interested Columbus

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:46.740
and other European readers of Marco Polo

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:52.640
was that the Grand Khan was
precisely not a Christian,

00:14:52.990 --> 00:14:57.070
but according to Marco
Polo wanted to be one.

00:14:57.070 --> 00:15:00.410
Although he had achieved
the heights of civilization

00:15:00.410 --> 00:15:02.590
without the benefits of Christianity,

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:04.400
the Grand Khan seemed to understand

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:08.130
the truth of the religion and
might even convert himself,

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:11.475
thereby gaining for Christianity

00:15:11.475 --> 00:15:12.975
the souls of all his subjects.

00:15:13.932 --> 00:15:16.970
This fantasy of the Grand Khan
and his potential conversion

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:19.940
lit European imaginations.

00:15:19.940 --> 00:15:22.820
For Christians he offered
the perfect mirror

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:25.930
of everything Islam should
have been but wasn't,

00:15:25.930 --> 00:15:27.730
a civilization no doubt,

00:15:27.730 --> 00:15:30.990
one formed outside of the
realm of Christianity,

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:33.800
but one that also recognized
its own bankruptcy

00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:35.973
in the face of the
glories of Christianity.

00:15:37.582 --> 00:15:40.120
The Grand Khan was thus
not yet a Christian,

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:43.563
but according to the fantasy
was always about to be one.

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:46.603
Moreover, once he entered the faith,

00:15:47.666 --> 00:15:50.849
the Muslims of the Middle East
in possession of Jerusalem,

00:15:50.849 --> 00:15:53.130
the object of the crusades,
would then be surrounded

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:57.460
by Christians who in one
final apocalyptic pincer move

00:15:57.460 --> 00:16:02.343
would destroy Islam forever
to retake the Holy city.

00:16:03.967 --> 00:16:05.830
So after centuries of crusades,

00:16:05.830 --> 00:16:09.111
with the spectacular growth of the Ottoman

00:16:09.111 --> 00:16:13.160
and Mamluk Empires
increasingly blocking access

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:17.540
to trade with the East, Europe
looked with anticipation

00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:20.943
to a potentate, thousands of miles away.

00:16:22.450 --> 00:16:25.290
Filled with these and other fantasies,

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:29.520
raised on accounts of the
crusades, Columbus's interactions

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:32.650
with Islam deepened
during his teenage years

00:16:32.650 --> 00:16:35.820
when he began taking to the Mediterranean

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:38.610
as an apprentice sailor,

00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:41.270
embarking on his first maritime voyages

00:16:41.270 --> 00:16:43.603
at the age of 13 or 14.

00:16:45.274 --> 00:16:47.640
He worked as a merchant's assistant,

00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:50.220
packing merchandise onboard ships,

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:52.680
recording weights and costs,

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:57.050
securing lodging in ports
for captain and crew,

00:16:57.050 --> 00:17:00.850
and generally being available
to help with navigation

00:17:00.850 --> 00:17:02.450
and business matters.

00:17:02.450 --> 00:17:04.320
He built a reputation for himself

00:17:04.320 --> 00:17:07.670
as a reliable and
efficient sailor for hire

00:17:07.670 --> 00:17:11.400
and was able to find endless
opportunities working the sea

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:15.170
for merchant and other expeditions

00:17:15.170 --> 00:17:18.590
in the employ of Europe's
many small principalities

00:17:18.590 --> 00:17:20.803
and larger polities.

00:17:22.211 --> 00:17:26.190
Some of these voyages in his early years

00:17:26.190 --> 00:17:27.890
sailing around the Mediterranean

00:17:28.871 --> 00:17:31.550
took him to the shores of
both the Ottoman Empire

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:34.323
and to other Muslim States around the sea.

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:39.300
So the map that I'm now showing you

00:17:40.140 --> 00:17:44.120
is one that we're quite
accustomed to seeing

00:17:44.120 --> 00:17:49.120
about Columbus's sailing
career before the four voyages,

00:17:52.571 --> 00:17:55.633
that he was plying off
the course of Portugal

00:17:55.633 --> 00:17:57.831
made his way down to West Africa,

00:17:57.831 --> 00:17:59.360
which I'll talk about in a moment.

00:17:59.360 --> 00:18:02.430
But the section that I'm
about to share with you

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:06.030
is really, even before
this, it's to make the point

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:09.580
that he had a Mediterranean sailing career

00:18:09.580 --> 00:18:13.080
before ever crossing through
the Strait of Gibraltar,

00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:16.150
and that this is an important
part of understanding Columbus

00:18:16.150 --> 00:18:18.640
and his biography, but really crucial

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:23.032
for understanding his connections
with the Muslim world.

00:18:23.032 --> 00:18:26.560
So I don't have a map of
his Mediterranean voyages,

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:29.710
but the map that I'm showing
you now has a pre-map

00:18:29.710 --> 00:18:32.623
that I'm gonna give to you now in words.

00:18:34.033 --> 00:18:39.033
So in 1472, when Columbus
was 21, we have his first,

00:18:41.210 --> 00:18:43.730
we have an account of his
first direct encounter

00:18:43.730 --> 00:18:47.071
with the Muslim world, the
first one that we know of.

00:18:47.071 --> 00:18:51.293
He took a job as a mercenary
sailor for the King of Anjou,

00:18:52.170 --> 00:18:55.250
during a war to capture Naples.

00:18:55.250 --> 00:18:57.270
Over the course of this fighting,

00:18:57.270 --> 00:18:59.840
one of the King's large three-masted ships

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:04.003
was captured and commandeered
to Tunis in North Africa.

00:19:05.100 --> 00:19:08.070
The King dispatched Columbus
to sail to North Africa

00:19:08.070 --> 00:19:09.703
to bring his ship back.

00:19:11.312 --> 00:19:13.490
Tunis in those years was the stronghold

00:19:13.490 --> 00:19:15.970
of the Hafsid Empire,

00:19:15.970 --> 00:19:20.970
an empire that ruled along much
of the coast of North Africa

00:19:21.490 --> 00:19:24.430
that made much of its money from piracy

00:19:24.430 --> 00:19:29.421
and also from the overland
caravan trade along the coast

00:19:29.421 --> 00:19:31.310
and then also along the Sahara.

00:19:31.310 --> 00:19:33.540
Like other city States
around the Mediterranean,

00:19:33.540 --> 00:19:37.050
whether in Italy or other
parts of North Africa,

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:41.890
the Hafsid Empire made a lot of money

00:19:41.890 --> 00:19:45.640
and was able to put that
money towards great arts,

00:19:45.640 --> 00:19:47.363
culture and patronage.

00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:50.450
Intrepid youth that he was,

00:19:50.450 --> 00:19:55.387
Columbus charged with retrieving
the King's ship sailed,

00:19:56.238 --> 00:20:00.910
excitedly he tells us, with
his more apprehensive crew

00:20:00.910 --> 00:20:05.730
from Naples first to
Sardinia to rest and resupply

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:08.930
and then turned south to North Africa

00:20:08.930 --> 00:20:10.530
to attempt to retrieve the ship.

00:20:11.380 --> 00:20:13.913
We don't know the outcome of this venture.

00:20:14.766 --> 00:20:16.100
We don't know if the ship
was retrieved or not.

00:20:16.100 --> 00:20:19.062
Columbus doesn't mention
that he retrieved the ship.

00:20:19.062 --> 00:20:22.590
One might presume that if he
had he would have mentioned it.

00:20:22.590 --> 00:20:25.950
Despite the uncertainties
though in the historical record,

00:20:25.950 --> 00:20:28.880
this earliest encounter of Columbus

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:32.600
with the world of Islam
was his first opportunity

00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:36.510
to test the stories
that he had heard about

00:20:36.510 --> 00:20:38.920
in his youth about crusades,

00:20:38.920 --> 00:20:41.950
the stories he'd heard at
church, from reading Marco Polo,

00:20:41.950 --> 00:20:44.470
his first opportunity
to test these stories

00:20:44.470 --> 00:20:49.470
against the reality of the
awesome power of a Muslim state,

00:20:49.602 --> 00:20:51.003
in this case, the Hafsid Empire.

00:20:52.023 --> 00:20:54.980
He experienced firsthand the naval power

00:20:54.980 --> 00:20:59.853
of the rulers of Tunis to
capture and hold the King's ship.

00:21:01.162 --> 00:21:04.580
His account that he writes after the fact

00:21:05.500 --> 00:21:09.660
describes the fear that
he saw in his crew's eyes

00:21:09.660 --> 00:21:12.143
when they realized they
would have to sail south

00:21:12.143 --> 00:21:15.210
into enemy territory,

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:18.723
presumably outmatched and out manned.

00:21:19.707 --> 00:21:21.790
Sailing to Tunis in his early twenties

00:21:21.790 --> 00:21:25.840
thus was an opportunity to test
the mythologies of his youth

00:21:25.840 --> 00:21:30.840
against the power of Muslim
States around the Mediterranean.

00:21:32.930 --> 00:21:35.820
He would gain closer
and much more profound

00:21:35.820 --> 00:21:38.846
firsthand experience of the Muslim world

00:21:38.846 --> 00:21:43.846
a couple years later when in
1475 a family firm in Genoa,

00:21:46.241 --> 00:21:48.870
the Spinolas, sent him to Chios,

00:21:48.870 --> 00:21:52.380
the hilly Island of
mostly scrub vegetation

00:21:52.380 --> 00:21:57.363
in the Aegean Sea, just
off the Anatolian shore.

00:21:58.422 --> 00:22:01.140
Chios famous for its mastic.

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:04.120
This is the mastic tree,
and then the mastic sap

00:22:04.120 --> 00:22:08.513
that was used in cooking
and medicines of all kinds.

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:15.340
Genoa lost its colony in Crimea
in the northern Black Sea,

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:22.700
its last outpost on the Black Sea in 1475,

00:22:22.940 --> 00:22:23.773
just a few months in fact

00:22:23.773 --> 00:22:26.960
before Columbus went to Chios that year.

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:30.560
So Chios when Columbus arrived there,

00:22:30.560 --> 00:22:34.460
represented Genoa's eastern most territory

00:22:35.566 --> 00:22:38.724
in the Mediterranean and the Black Sea,

00:22:38.724 --> 00:22:40.480
and was therefore of vital commercial

00:22:40.480 --> 00:22:43.503
and strategic interests.

00:22:45.121 --> 00:22:47.221
This is a rendition of the ports of Chios.

00:22:48.784 --> 00:22:51.180
In traveling to Chios,
Columbus was exposed

00:22:51.180 --> 00:22:52.950
for the very first time

00:22:52.950 --> 00:22:55.503
to the awesome power
of the Ottoman Empire.

00:22:56.410 --> 00:22:59.140
Although the Island was
still a Genoese colony,

00:22:59.140 --> 00:23:02.080
it was very much in the Ottoman's orbit.

00:23:02.080 --> 00:23:04.980
They ruled the blue seas around the island

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:07.990
and all the ports of call on the coast

00:23:07.990 --> 00:23:11.090
just across the narrow straits
that separated the island

00:23:11.090 --> 00:23:12.763
from mainland Anatolia.

00:23:14.302 --> 00:23:16.810
The Ottomans had captured
other major Aegean islands

00:23:16.810 --> 00:23:18.970
over the previous two decades,

00:23:18.970 --> 00:23:23.970
places like Imbros,
Lemnos, Lesbos, Samothrace,

00:23:26.582 --> 00:23:29.810
and Columbus was regaled when in Chios

00:23:29.810 --> 00:23:32.020
with tales of dread and horror

00:23:33.002 --> 00:23:36.070
about the Ottoman conquest
of Caffa in Crimea,

00:23:36.070 --> 00:23:38.663
again just a few months
before he arrived there,

00:23:39.740 --> 00:23:42.440
and even more so of the
conquest of Constantinople

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:45.380
almost two decades earlier.

00:23:45.380 --> 00:23:49.500
The memory of the loss of
Constantinople in particular

00:23:49.500 --> 00:23:53.041
was very fresh on the island of Chios,

00:23:53.041 --> 00:23:55.470
even over 20 years later.

00:23:55.470 --> 00:23:59.330
The Genoese sent many
reinforcements from Chios,

00:23:59.330 --> 00:24:03.458
the closest of the
Italian State's outposts

00:24:03.458 --> 00:24:04.970
to Constantinople,

00:24:04.970 --> 00:24:07.290
and this contingent suffered heavy losses

00:24:07.290 --> 00:24:11.203
that affected almost
every family on Chios.

00:24:12.500 --> 00:24:14.740
So the stories Columbus
learned in his youth

00:24:14.740 --> 00:24:17.510
of Christianity's crusades with Islam

00:24:17.510 --> 00:24:19.740
were suddenly brought to life for him.

00:24:19.740 --> 00:24:23.220
When he met and heard from
the relatives of others

00:24:23.220 --> 00:24:26.740
who had actually fought
against the awesome power

00:24:26.740 --> 00:24:30.653
of the Ottomans in their
conquest of Constantinople.

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:33.680
Here on the Eastern front of Christendom,

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:36.950
he witnessed the awesome
power of the Ottoman Empire.

00:24:36.950 --> 00:24:40.880
He saw that it surrounded
a tiny island like Chios,

00:24:40.880 --> 00:24:45.360
that it might very well
continue its march west,

00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:48.350
perhaps even into Europe itself.

00:24:48.350 --> 00:24:52.750
Like other islands, the
majority of Chios was Greek,

00:24:52.750 --> 00:24:56.523
but there was a growing Turkish
population there as well.

00:24:57.710 --> 00:25:02.710
Columbus heard on the
island about Constantinople

00:25:02.980 --> 00:25:07.850
and other losses around the
Black Sea and in the Aegean,

00:25:07.850 --> 00:25:10.070
bringing home to him again the idea

00:25:10.070 --> 00:25:14.530
that Christianity had to be
boldly offensive against Islam

00:25:14.530 --> 00:25:19.530
or risk further losses to the Ottomans

00:25:20.400 --> 00:25:21.623
and other Muslim powers.

00:25:23.080 --> 00:25:26.640
After his time in the
employ of the Spinolas

00:25:26.640 --> 00:25:28.900
and his time in Chios,

00:25:28.900 --> 00:25:31.770
Columbus eventually makes
his way to Portugal.

00:25:31.770 --> 00:25:36.200
There he fell in with
the group of explorers

00:25:36.200 --> 00:25:40.260
that the famous Portuguese
Prince Henry the Navigator

00:25:40.260 --> 00:25:42.083
had assembled before his death.

00:25:43.140 --> 00:25:45.400
Like many ambitious Europeans,

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:47.260
these Portuguese adventurers

00:25:47.260 --> 00:25:50.563
were looking for ways
around the Muslim world.

00:25:52.018 --> 00:25:54.540
They also believed in
the truth of crusade.

00:25:54.540 --> 00:25:57.490
Like Columbus they had heard tales

00:25:57.490 --> 00:26:00.780
of the Grand Khan of the
East and were interested

00:26:00.780 --> 00:26:05.780
in trying to find a path
towards the Grand Khan.

00:26:05.960 --> 00:26:07.790
They were indeed quite explicit

00:26:07.790 --> 00:26:12.250
in defining many of their
expeditions in religious terms,

00:26:12.250 --> 00:26:16.303
as part of a global crusade
between Christendom and Islam.

00:26:17.939 --> 00:26:21.230
For example, according to one
of Henry's contemporaries,

00:26:21.230 --> 00:26:24.033
writing in that fateful
year 1453, Henry quote

00:26:27.427 --> 00:26:29.733
"was actuated by the zeal for God,

00:26:31.140 --> 00:26:33.218
"by the disease desire for Alliance

00:26:33.218 --> 00:26:35.459
"with the Eastern Christians
of the Grand Khan,

00:26:35.459 --> 00:26:37.397
"by an eagerness to know how far the power

00:26:37.397 --> 00:26:42.013
"of the infidel existed, by
the wish to convert people

00:26:42.013 --> 00:26:44.763
"to Christianity and by the
desire to fight the Moors."

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:50.345
Henry's most famous plan
to outflank the Muslims

00:26:50.345 --> 00:26:53.350
of the Mediterranean rested on his belief

00:26:53.350 --> 00:26:55.923
in a Southern route around Africa,

00:26:57.290 --> 00:27:00.660
and he and his explorers
spent much of their time

00:27:00.660 --> 00:27:05.240
playing along the West African coast.

00:27:05.240 --> 00:27:08.010
It was to their utter terror and dismay

00:27:08.010 --> 00:27:11.100
when working their way South
along the African coast,

00:27:11.100 --> 00:27:14.420
they discovered that Islam
was already flourishing

00:27:14.420 --> 00:27:17.650
in many parts of West Africa.

00:27:17.650 --> 00:27:19.840
By the time the Portuguese arrived,

00:27:19.840 --> 00:27:23.780
Islam had grown from a minority
faith of a few adherents

00:27:23.780 --> 00:27:25.400
to the official state religion

00:27:25.400 --> 00:27:29.720
of several West African empires,
most prominent among them

00:27:29.720 --> 00:27:33.448
the Mali empire that had adopted Islam

00:27:33.448 --> 00:27:35.683
as its official religion in 1324.

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:40.790
In the 15th century when
these Portuguese explorers

00:27:40.790 --> 00:27:44.110
were sailing along the coast,
the presence of Muslims

00:27:44.110 --> 00:27:47.756
in West Africa further deepened the sense

00:27:47.756 --> 00:27:50.380
among European Christian
Christian crusaders

00:27:50.380 --> 00:27:53.700
that their faith was
somehow surrounded by Islam

00:27:53.700 --> 00:27:58.700
and that some drastic action
was needed to attempt to defend

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:00.773
against this further spread.

00:28:01.990 --> 00:28:06.100
As they continued sailing
south along the African coast,

00:28:06.100 --> 00:28:08.630
the farthest point they reached

00:28:08.630 --> 00:28:12.593
was the port city of São Jorge da Mina,

00:28:13.949 --> 00:28:15.899
what is known today as Elmina in Ghana.

00:28:17.120 --> 00:28:20.960
This city would prove a
linchpin of Portugal's empire

00:28:20.960 --> 00:28:25.960
in West Africa, a key conduit
for funneling first gold

00:28:26.040 --> 00:28:30.120
and later slaves from
West Africa to Portugal

00:28:30.120 --> 00:28:32.483
and then eventually elsewhere as well.

00:28:33.610 --> 00:28:38.080
In 1481, Portugal's new King João II

00:28:38.080 --> 00:28:41.540
resolved that his kingdom
needed a stronger presence

00:28:41.540 --> 00:28:46.080
on the Gulf of Guinea to protect
its growing economic stakes

00:28:46.080 --> 00:28:47.830
in the gold trade.

00:28:47.830 --> 00:28:51.170
And so he dispatched ahead
a heavily armed fleet

00:28:52.365 --> 00:28:55.310
with building materials
to this trading post

00:28:55.310 --> 00:29:00.093
of São Jorge da Mina to
construct a fortress.

00:29:01.590 --> 00:29:05.090
On 10 caravels and two transport ships,

00:29:05.090 --> 00:29:09.330
foundation stones, roof
tiles, nails, provisions,

00:29:09.330 --> 00:29:13.020
all were sent, and the
structure that was constructed

00:29:13.020 --> 00:29:17.310
was one of the first European buildings

00:29:17.310 --> 00:29:20.210
constructed in Sub-Saharan Africa.

00:29:20.210 --> 00:29:24.360
Of course, many men were
sent on these 12 ships

00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:26.000
to help with the construction

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:29.163
and to deal with any business matters.

00:29:30.030 --> 00:29:35.030
And Columbus was on one of these
12 ships, now 30 years old.

00:29:37.574 --> 00:29:41.810
When he arrives in Elmina,
Columbus brings together

00:29:41.810 --> 00:29:44.400
three pieces of very crucial information

00:29:45.498 --> 00:29:48.732
that concretize the idea that
he had been thinking about

00:29:48.732 --> 00:29:52.640
for quite a while now, the idea
of a Western route to Asia.

00:29:53.890 --> 00:29:57.750
In West Africa, he found
seemingly endless supplies

00:29:57.750 --> 00:30:00.850
of precious gold, the gold coast.

00:30:00.850 --> 00:30:04.760
Contemporary understandings
of geography taught Columbus

00:30:04.760 --> 00:30:08.860
that regions at the same
latitude enjoyed similar climates

00:30:08.860 --> 00:30:10.470
and natural features.

00:30:10.470 --> 00:30:14.900
So in his understanding, if he
was to head directly due West

00:30:14.900 --> 00:30:18.530
from Elmina, he would
find lands rich in gold.

00:30:18.530 --> 00:30:22.750
Gold was important to him
to help to build the army

00:30:22.750 --> 00:30:26.083
that he needed for the
reconquest of Jerusalem.

00:30:27.530 --> 00:30:29.040
The second piece of information

00:30:29.040 --> 00:30:34.040
that Columbus learned in
Elmina was that Asia was close.

00:30:34.220 --> 00:30:37.350
Sailors in the African city told him

00:30:37.350 --> 00:30:40.570
that logs regularly washed up on shore

00:30:40.570 --> 00:30:44.630
that seemed to have been
worked by human hands.

00:30:44.630 --> 00:30:47.660
Evidence that the lands across the ocean

00:30:47.660 --> 00:30:51.090
from whence this worked
wood supposedly came

00:30:51.090 --> 00:30:54.023
could not have been very far away at all.

00:30:56.116 --> 00:30:59.230
The third piece of information
that he learned in Elmina

00:31:00.174 --> 00:31:05.174
had to do with the Atlantic's
currents and wind patterns.

00:31:05.490 --> 00:31:08.730
The prevailing current
off the West African coast

00:31:08.730 --> 00:31:12.219
flows out to sea which
is the exact opposite

00:31:12.219 --> 00:31:14.770
of the situation in Portugal

00:31:14.770 --> 00:31:18.120
where the eastward currents
made it difficult to sail West

00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:19.363
for most of the year.

00:31:20.814 --> 00:31:22.840
So to head West from Iberia then,

00:31:22.840 --> 00:31:26.860
it made sense to first sail
South along the African coast

00:31:26.860 --> 00:31:31.283
to pick up the westerly
current across the ocean.

00:31:32.650 --> 00:31:36.150
From Elmina Columbus returned to Iberia,

00:31:36.150 --> 00:31:40.520
confident that he understood
now how to execute his plan

00:31:40.520 --> 00:31:45.520
to sail across the ocean to
find Asia, the Grand Khan,

00:31:47.900 --> 00:31:50.740
to surround the Muslims of the Middle East

00:31:50.740 --> 00:31:53.373
and eventually to conquer Jerusalem.

00:31:54.310 --> 00:31:57.173
The thing that he most needed
at this point was a patron.

00:31:58.070 --> 00:32:01.330
The story of Columbus's
back and forth negotiations

00:32:01.330 --> 00:32:05.020
between various European sovereigns

00:32:05.020 --> 00:32:07.290
to secure patronage is
a very, very famous one

00:32:07.290 --> 00:32:11.690
that I won't get into now.

00:32:11.690 --> 00:32:12.930
What I want to emphasize though,

00:32:12.930 --> 00:32:17.790
and what has not been
remarked upon is the role

00:32:17.790 --> 00:32:20.860
that the Ottomans and other Muslims played

00:32:20.860 --> 00:32:23.160
in this affair of seeking patronage.

00:32:23.160 --> 00:32:25.440
Columbus's major push for the money

00:32:25.440 --> 00:32:28.480
that he needed for his voyages occurred

00:32:28.480 --> 00:32:31.280
during the late 1480s.

00:32:31.280 --> 00:32:34.940
This was the same decade in
which the eventual financiers

00:32:34.940 --> 00:32:38.290
of his voyages, the second
cousins Isabella and Ferdinand

00:32:39.150 --> 00:32:44.150
had declared their war
against Islam in Iberia.

00:32:44.330 --> 00:32:48.570
And it's not a coincidence
that Europe's war on Islam

00:32:48.570 --> 00:32:53.570
in Iberia and elsewhere and
Columbus's voyages occurred

00:32:53.600 --> 00:32:55.740
at almost exactly the same time.

00:32:55.740 --> 00:32:59.650
In Columbus's mind, in the
mind of the Spanish sovereigns,

00:32:59.650 --> 00:33:02.220
these were two pieces
of what they interpreted

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:06.140
as a millennial war between
Christianity and Islam.

00:33:06.140 --> 00:33:09.730
As part of a global
strategy against Islam,

00:33:09.730 --> 00:33:13.085
the Spanish moved their navies East

00:33:13.085 --> 00:33:14.313
in the Mediterranean

00:33:14.313 --> 00:33:16.290
against the Ottomans' other Muslim powers,

00:33:16.290 --> 00:33:19.270
endeavored to push out
all of Iberia's Muslims,

00:33:19.270 --> 00:33:22.580
and sent Columbus off to the West

00:33:22.580 --> 00:33:25.283
to try to surround the
Muslims of the Middle East.

00:33:26.660 --> 00:33:29.770
Ottoman advances in the second
half of the 15th century

00:33:29.770 --> 00:33:33.283
directly impacted Spanish
holdings in the Mediterranean.

00:33:34.976 --> 00:33:36.020
Mehmed the conqueror,

00:33:36.020 --> 00:33:38.670
the conqueror of Constantinople,

00:33:38.670 --> 00:33:43.670
captured the strategic strategic
walled port city of Otranto

00:33:45.282 --> 00:33:49.150
on the very tip of the heel
of the Italian peninsula,

00:33:49.150 --> 00:33:50.963
right here Otranto.

00:33:51.810 --> 00:33:56.623
He captured this small port in 1480.

00:33:58.472 --> 00:34:03.320
From there he sent ships
to the coast of Sicily

00:34:03.320 --> 00:34:07.083
to assess a potential
invasion of that island.

00:34:08.692 --> 00:34:10.530
Sicily is the largest of
the Mediterranean's island,

00:34:10.530 --> 00:34:12.383
once a Muslim possession, of course,

00:34:13.673 --> 00:34:16.950
but in the 15th century was
under the nominal sovereignty

00:34:16.950 --> 00:34:19.720
of Ferdinand and Isabella of Spain.

00:34:19.720 --> 00:34:23.440
And so a threatening move by the Ottomans

00:34:23.440 --> 00:34:27.490
demanded the Spanish King
and Queen's attention.

00:34:27.490 --> 00:34:30.840
Ottoman rule over a place like Sicily

00:34:30.840 --> 00:34:32.910
would have solidified their control

00:34:32.910 --> 00:34:36.140
of the entire Eastern
half of the Mediterranean.

00:34:36.140 --> 00:34:39.810
Again, think of Genoa being
pushed out of the Black Sea,

00:34:39.810 --> 00:34:41.990
being steadily pushed out of the Aegean.

00:34:41.990 --> 00:34:44.570
And now the Ottomans
pushing even further West,

00:34:44.570 --> 00:34:46.993
potentially all the way to Sicily.

00:34:48.720 --> 00:34:50.250
Were the Ottomans to hold Sicily,

00:34:50.250 --> 00:34:51.710
this would give them a launching pad

00:34:51.710 --> 00:34:54.323
for invasions further West in the sea.

00:34:55.280 --> 00:34:58.660
Sicily is very much the
midway point of the sea

00:34:58.660 --> 00:35:02.453
and was of much concern of the Ottoman,

00:35:04.840 --> 00:35:06.420
of the Spanish sovereigns.

00:35:06.420 --> 00:35:10.420
So much so that one of
Isabella's court chroniclers

00:35:10.420 --> 00:35:14.777
wrote quote, "Every day news
came to the King and Queen

00:35:14.777 --> 00:35:17.857
"that the Turks had a
great Armada on the sea

00:35:17.857 --> 00:35:20.057
"and that they were sending it to conquer

00:35:20.057 --> 00:35:21.517
"the kingdom of Sicily."

00:35:22.960 --> 00:35:24.820
The Spanish sovereigns leaped into action,

00:35:24.820 --> 00:35:27.420
sending a fleet to defend Sicily.

00:35:27.420 --> 00:35:32.420
Luckily for them though Mehmed
died the next year in 1481.

00:35:32.450 --> 00:35:36.560
And the Ottoman succession
crisis forced the empire

00:35:36.560 --> 00:35:41.560
to give up on Sicily, to give up Otronto,

00:35:41.618 --> 00:35:44.280
and indeed to withdraw
from Italy altogether,

00:35:44.280 --> 00:35:47.080
and they would never
gain any other territory

00:35:47.080 --> 00:35:49.653
on the Italian peninsula after that.

00:35:50.640 --> 00:35:54.520
Despite this retreat though,
over the course of the 1480s,

00:35:54.520 --> 00:35:57.320
the Ottomans continued to push West

00:35:57.320 --> 00:36:00.860
in the Mediterranean
testing Spain's resolve

00:36:00.860 --> 00:36:03.223
and the resolve of other European powers.

00:36:05.177 --> 00:36:10.177
In 1488, Mehmed's son and
successor Bayezid attacked Malta.

00:36:12.210 --> 00:36:15.220
This prompted Spain again
to send reinforcements

00:36:15.220 --> 00:36:17.860
to strengthen its garrisons in Sicily.

00:36:17.860 --> 00:36:20.503
Again, Malta very close
to Sicily as you see here.

00:36:21.840 --> 00:36:25.170
Between 1487 and the middle of the 1490s,

00:36:25.170 --> 00:36:28.110
Ottoman ships made
landings even further West

00:36:28.110 --> 00:36:32.850
reaching all the way to Corsica,
Pisa, the Baelaric Islands,

00:36:32.850 --> 00:36:34.850
and North African ports

00:36:34.850 --> 00:36:39.060
just across from the
Iberian mainland itself.

00:36:39.060 --> 00:36:41.670
These were not full-scale
military invasions,

00:36:41.670 --> 00:36:46.280
did not result in any sort of
lasting Ottoman settlements,

00:36:46.280 --> 00:36:48.760
but nevertheless, the
sight of Ottoman ships

00:36:48.760 --> 00:36:51.330
forcibly pulling in to harbor

00:36:51.330 --> 00:36:56.263
panicked already
frightened European States.

00:36:57.121 --> 00:36:59.921
The force of the Ottomans
seemed to be all over the sea.

00:37:01.040 --> 00:37:03.910
These gains in the
Mediterranean against Spain

00:37:03.910 --> 00:37:05.940
and other European interests

00:37:05.940 --> 00:37:09.690
made the already precarious
position of Spain's Muslims

00:37:09.690 --> 00:37:10.713
much more dire.

00:37:11.700 --> 00:37:14.880
These internal Muslims in
Iberia were lumped together

00:37:15.740 --> 00:37:17.459
with Spain's external Muslim enemies.

00:37:17.459 --> 00:37:21.000
Like Columbus, Henry, the Pope,

00:37:21.000 --> 00:37:24.780
Isabella considered it her
duty to crusade against Muslims

00:37:24.780 --> 00:37:26.343
wherever they may be.

00:37:27.431 --> 00:37:28.750
Within Iberia there were Muslims

00:37:28.750 --> 00:37:31.890
both living under Spanish Christian rule

00:37:31.890 --> 00:37:36.775
and small Muslim States in the south,

00:37:36.775 --> 00:37:39.074
independent states in the south.

00:37:39.074 --> 00:37:43.972
Both were liable allies of the
Ottomans and other Muslims,

00:37:43.972 --> 00:37:46.510
according to Isabella and Ferdinand.

00:37:46.510 --> 00:37:50.210
So given the Ottoman's territorial
expansion in the 1480s,

00:37:50.210 --> 00:37:54.499
Isabella and Ferdinand saw
their Reconquista in the South,

00:37:54.499 --> 00:37:55.980
their war against the Muslims in the south

00:37:55.980 --> 00:38:00.490
not simply as a process of
local political consolidation

00:38:00.490 --> 00:38:02.250
on the Iberian peninsula,

00:38:02.250 --> 00:38:05.459
but rather as one piece of what they saw

00:38:05.459 --> 00:38:07.230
as a global civilizational war.

00:38:07.230 --> 00:38:09.740
Interpreted by them
and by other Christians

00:38:09.740 --> 00:38:13.010
as the first step in rolling back Islam

00:38:13.010 --> 00:38:16.590
and its representative states
from the Mediterranean,

00:38:16.590 --> 00:38:18.650
and if they had their way eventually

00:38:18.650 --> 00:38:20.873
from the face of the earth altogether.

00:38:22.200 --> 00:38:25.650
Steeped his entire life in
the rhetoric and reality

00:38:25.650 --> 00:38:28.560
of the crusades, Columbus used his belief

00:38:28.560 --> 00:38:32.510
in the notion of a war
between Christendom and Islam

00:38:32.510 --> 00:38:36.075
to push his case for the Atlantic voyages

00:38:36.075 --> 00:38:37.440
with Isabella and Ferdinand.

00:38:37.440 --> 00:38:41.610
If he could show them that
heading West across the ocean

00:38:41.610 --> 00:38:44.210
would help to defeat
Islam, then he thought

00:38:44.210 --> 00:38:48.540
they might be more liable
to finance his journey.

00:38:48.540 --> 00:38:51.130
Columbus found it very
difficult to get any traction

00:38:51.130 --> 00:38:52.870
with the sovereigns in this period.

00:38:52.870 --> 00:38:55.560
They were really too busy with their Wars

00:38:55.560 --> 00:38:57.840
to pay him any attention.

00:38:57.840 --> 00:39:02.840
In the late 1480s, Ferdinand
and Isabella were fighting

00:39:03.240 --> 00:39:07.450
in the south, and the
Reconquista was quite successful

00:39:07.450 --> 00:39:09.323
in these years.

00:39:10.870 --> 00:39:13.720
The famous surrender
of Granada, of course,

00:39:13.720 --> 00:39:18.720
on January 2nd, 1492, legally
ended over seven centuries

00:39:19.430 --> 00:39:22.533
of Muslim rule on the Iberian peninsula.

00:39:24.020 --> 00:39:26.650
For Christians everywhere,
not just in Spain,

00:39:26.650 --> 00:39:30.380
Granada represented a
monumental victory over Islam.

00:39:30.380 --> 00:39:33.780
Church bells rang out
across European cities.

00:39:33.780 --> 00:39:36.400
The Pope ordered a day of festivities.

00:39:36.400 --> 00:39:40.290
Celebratory bull fights
were organized across Spain.

00:39:40.290 --> 00:39:44.630
Mock reenactments of the siege
of Granada were played out

00:39:44.630 --> 00:39:47.470
across European towns.

00:39:47.470 --> 00:39:51.620
Coming just a few decades after
the loss of Constantinople,

00:39:51.620 --> 00:39:55.590
Granada was for many in
Europe an act of retribution,

00:39:55.590 --> 00:39:56.900
a Christian answer

00:39:56.900 --> 00:40:00.690
to the most powerful
Muslim empire on earth.

00:40:00.690 --> 00:40:03.570
In the words of one
Spanish court historian,

00:40:03.570 --> 00:40:06.457
the Conquest of Granada quote
"was famed and celebrated

00:40:06.457 --> 00:40:08.527
"through all the realms of Christendom,

00:40:08.527 --> 00:40:11.557
"and it extended to the
farthest and most remote lands

00:40:11.557 --> 00:40:13.617
"of the Turk and the Sultan."

00:40:15.660 --> 00:40:19.350
After the fall of
Granada in January, 1492,

00:40:19.350 --> 00:40:23.540
Isabella, against the
judgment of her advisors

00:40:23.540 --> 00:40:27.500
and her husband, emboldened
by her recent victories,

00:40:27.500 --> 00:40:31.760
agreed to fund Columbus's
journey across the Atlantic.

00:40:31.760 --> 00:40:36.360
She had just vanquished
Spain's Muslim kingdoms,

00:40:36.360 --> 00:40:41.130
a first step she thought in her global war

00:40:41.130 --> 00:40:43.920
against Christianity's ultimate enemy.

00:40:43.920 --> 00:40:46.830
Christ would March upon the world.

00:40:46.830 --> 00:40:48.580
Jerusalem would be won.

00:40:48.580 --> 00:40:53.000
If Columbus's plan, risky
and farfetched as it was,

00:40:53.000 --> 00:40:55.395
could contribute in even a minor way

00:40:55.395 --> 00:40:58.780
to the destruction of Islam,
then it would have proved

00:40:58.780 --> 00:41:02.513
more than a worthy investment
of the Queen's resources.

00:41:04.130 --> 00:41:06.530
The opening lines of Columbus's log book

00:41:06.530 --> 00:41:09.770
on his first foray across
the Atlantic make plain

00:41:09.770 --> 00:41:13.210
that he understood his
voyage as a new crusade

00:41:13.210 --> 00:41:17.003
in centuries of war between
Islam and Christendom.

00:41:19.570 --> 00:41:22.570
In the space of just a single sentence,

00:41:22.570 --> 00:41:25.400
Columbus pivoted from Spain's Reconquista

00:41:26.450 --> 00:41:29.854
to finding and converting the Grand Khan

00:41:29.854 --> 00:41:34.854
Marco Polo had promised and to
surrounding therefore Islam.

00:41:39.810 --> 00:41:44.180
The extension of Spain's
civilizational religious crusade

00:41:44.180 --> 00:41:48.380
is part of what sends Columbus
off across the Atlantic,

00:41:48.380 --> 00:41:51.640
and this is essential to
understanding his voyages.

00:41:51.640 --> 00:41:54.350
He sailed off to win Jerusalem.

00:41:54.350 --> 00:41:58.200
He sailed off to win
Jerusalem for Christendom.

00:41:58.200 --> 00:42:01.770
And this is why the story
that I've relayed to you

00:42:01.770 --> 00:42:05.230
of the biography of Columbus's
interactions with Islam

00:42:05.230 --> 00:42:06.683
matters so much.

00:42:07.572 --> 00:42:08.820
From his earliest days in Genoa

00:42:08.820 --> 00:42:11.470
where he waved good tidings to crusaders

00:42:11.470 --> 00:42:15.420
to his experience with the
Portuguese enslavement of Muslims

00:42:15.420 --> 00:42:17.630
and others in West Africa

00:42:17.630 --> 00:42:21.350
to the very moment before
he left Europe in Granada,

00:42:21.350 --> 00:42:25.393
Columbus's life was formed
in and against Islam.

00:42:27.780 --> 00:42:32.780
So this brings us then to
that fateful moment, to 1492,

00:42:32.780 --> 00:42:34.360
that freighted tipping point

00:42:35.972 --> 00:42:38.590
of multi-continental
destruction and transformation.

00:42:38.590 --> 00:42:43.590
It also brings me to stop
sharing my screen now.

00:42:48.070 --> 00:42:51.050
What does the history I've just relayed

00:42:51.050 --> 00:42:54.670
of Columbus and Islam
mean for our understanding

00:42:54.670 --> 00:42:56.380
of this fateful year?

00:42:57.550 --> 00:43:00.880
When turning to the writings
of Columbus and others

00:43:00.880 --> 00:43:04.460
in that first generation of conquistadors,

00:43:04.460 --> 00:43:09.410
one finds perhaps surprisingly
sparks of Islam flying up

00:43:09.410 --> 00:43:11.340
from their pages.

00:43:11.340 --> 00:43:15.650
In the Americas where Islam
so very clearly did not exist,

00:43:15.650 --> 00:43:17.680
they nevertheless saw it.

00:43:17.680 --> 00:43:21.290
As arresting as it may
seem, the Spanish stretched

00:43:21.290 --> 00:43:25.560
the analytical limits of their
late 15th century universe

00:43:25.560 --> 00:43:27.950
to understand some of the Americas

00:43:27.950 --> 00:43:32.950
and its indigenous peoples
as somehow related to Islam.

00:43:33.320 --> 00:43:34.390
What do I mean by this?

00:43:34.390 --> 00:43:36.253
Let me give some examples.

00:43:38.030 --> 00:43:42.280
In his journals, Columbus
refers to the weapons

00:43:42.280 --> 00:43:47.280
of the indigenous Tainos in
the Caribbean as el fanjes,

00:43:48.060 --> 00:43:50.810
a Spanish word derived from the Arabic

00:43:52.093 --> 00:43:55.110
for a curved metal sword
inscribed with Koranic versus

00:43:55.110 --> 00:43:58.693
that was commonly used by Muslim soldiers

00:43:58.693 --> 00:44:00.563
in battle in Spain.

00:44:02.176 --> 00:44:04.920
Despite the fact that
Columbus himself tells us

00:44:04.920 --> 00:44:07.447
that the Taino quote "have no iron."

00:44:08.712 --> 00:44:11.020
And of course they knew
nothing of the Koran.

00:44:11.020 --> 00:44:13.300
He likens them to Muslim soldiers

00:44:13.300 --> 00:44:17.920
by putting these swords in his
hands, in their hands, sorry.

00:44:17.920 --> 00:44:20.480
Later when Columbus first saw the scarves

00:44:20.480 --> 00:44:23.360
of a group of Taino women,

00:44:23.360 --> 00:44:26.300
he thought they were somehow
related through trade

00:44:26.300 --> 00:44:29.080
or some other form of Eurasian contact

00:44:29.080 --> 00:44:32.023
to what he termed Moorish sashes.

00:44:32.950 --> 00:44:35.800
A friar named Ramón Pané

00:44:35.800 --> 00:44:39.697
who sailed on Columbus's
second voyage in 1494

00:44:41.400 --> 00:44:46.400
described Indian singing as
akin to that of the Moors.

00:44:46.940 --> 00:44:50.960
A couple decades later, Cortés wrote

00:44:50.960 --> 00:44:55.550
that the Aztecs of Mexico
quote "wore Moorish robes,"

00:44:55.550 --> 00:45:00.090
that Aztec women quote
"looked like Moorish women."

00:45:00.090 --> 00:45:03.130
Aztec dancing he thought
was derived from the dances

00:45:03.130 --> 00:45:03.963
of the Moors.

00:45:05.040 --> 00:45:07.340
Cortés's encounter with the Aztecs

00:45:07.340 --> 00:45:10.650
proved especially fertile for
these kinds of comparisons

00:45:10.650 --> 00:45:14.790
between the Islamic civilizations
the Spanish had fought

00:45:14.790 --> 00:45:17.680
in the old world and what
they were experiencing

00:45:17.680 --> 00:45:18.563
in the new world.

00:45:19.807 --> 00:45:21.580
In the Aztecs, Cortés and his men

00:45:21.580 --> 00:45:25.720
met a formidable, complex
empire with enormous cities,

00:45:25.720 --> 00:45:27.930
one of the largest cities
in the world at the time,

00:45:27.930 --> 00:45:30.170
a highly sophisticated political culture,

00:45:30.170 --> 00:45:31.863
and imposing armies.

00:45:32.820 --> 00:45:35.920
More than, for example, Taino society,

00:45:35.920 --> 00:45:38.620
the Spanish saw in the Aztec's arrival

00:45:38.620 --> 00:45:43.030
and in the civilization
a kin to their enemies

00:45:43.030 --> 00:45:47.350
of the old world, the Muslim
empires of the Mediterranean.

00:45:47.350 --> 00:45:49.950
Cortés wrote, for example, that he saw

00:45:49.950 --> 00:45:54.950
over 400 mosques, mezquitas,
in what is today in Mexico.

00:45:55.330 --> 00:45:57.820
He called Montezuma a Sultan.

00:45:57.820 --> 00:45:59.870
He described Montezuma's palaces

00:45:59.870 --> 00:46:02.100
in terms strikingly similar

00:46:02.100 --> 00:46:05.550
to those of the Muslim palaces of Granada

00:46:05.550 --> 00:46:08.300
including claiming to see in Mexico

00:46:08.300 --> 00:46:13.300
tiles and roofing that only
existed in Granada at the time.

00:46:15.210 --> 00:46:19.340
These things continued long
after Columbus and Cortés.

00:46:19.340 --> 00:46:22.140
Over the course of the
16th century, the Spanish,

00:46:22.140 --> 00:46:25.220
for example, would try
to subdue and convert

00:46:25.220 --> 00:46:28.870
the nomadic Chichimecas of Mexico,

00:46:28.870 --> 00:46:33.200
referring to them as árabes, the Arabs.

00:46:33.200 --> 00:46:37.350
In Brazil, the children of
Portuguese men and Indian women

00:46:37.350 --> 00:46:41.768
were referred to as el
Mamelucos, the Mamluks,

00:46:41.768 --> 00:46:43.410
the Mamluk empire based in Cairo.

00:46:43.410 --> 00:46:46.900
Plains Indians captured and
transported by the Spanish

00:46:46.900 --> 00:46:49.850
as slaves to what is today New Mexico

00:46:49.850 --> 00:46:52.370
came to be known as genízaros,

00:46:52.370 --> 00:46:54.390
a corruption of the Turkish word

00:46:54.390 --> 00:46:56.493
for the Janissaries, yeñiçeri.

00:46:57.470 --> 00:47:02.096
The Spanish named a large city
in the Yucatan Grand Cairo.

00:47:02.096 --> 00:47:05.860
We could go on and on citing
these kinds of examples.

00:47:05.860 --> 00:47:09.610
The city on the US Mexico border today

00:47:09.610 --> 00:47:12.500
that's in the news because
of the migrant crisis

00:47:12.500 --> 00:47:16.590
is another example, this
Matamoros, Moor Slayer,

00:47:16.590 --> 00:47:17.953
the Slayer of the Moors.

00:47:20.173 --> 00:47:23.520
On one level there's nothing
surprising about any of this.

00:47:23.520 --> 00:47:25.760
The Spanish conquistadors were forged

00:47:25.760 --> 00:47:29.740
in an old world crucible of
Christian Muslim violence.

00:47:29.740 --> 00:47:32.300
Many of them had fought
against the Muslim kingdoms

00:47:32.300 --> 00:47:35.760
of Southern Liberia and North Africa.

00:47:35.760 --> 00:47:39.920
Many had encountered Muslims
in West Africa as well.

00:47:39.920 --> 00:47:43.630
Many had traded with
Muslim traders in ports

00:47:44.494 --> 00:47:45.327
around the Mediterranean.

00:47:45.327 --> 00:47:47.830
Grasping for some conceptual framework

00:47:47.830 --> 00:47:50.640
through which to reconcile
all of the difference

00:47:50.640 --> 00:47:53.660
and the newness they
encountered in the Americas,

00:47:53.660 --> 00:47:56.560
they turned to the most
extreme version of otherness

00:47:56.560 --> 00:47:58.683
available to them, Islam.

00:47:59.530 --> 00:48:01.600
In a new place, fighting new enemies,

00:48:01.600 --> 00:48:04.653
they fell back on what
they knew, as we always do.

00:48:05.860 --> 00:48:08.751
As John Elliott, one of
the foremost historians

00:48:08.751 --> 00:48:11.947
of Cortés notes quote, "Since
the only political model

00:48:11.947 --> 00:48:16.230
"for his conquest Cortés
knew was the reconquest,"

00:48:16.230 --> 00:48:17.737
the Reconquista,

00:48:17.737 --> 00:48:20.327
"Montezuma had to be
treated in his narrative

00:48:20.327 --> 00:48:22.573
"as a Muslim ruler would have been.

00:48:23.867 --> 00:48:26.747
"Anáhuac in central
Mexico is thus described

00:48:26.747 --> 00:48:30.327
"as far as Cortés is able
in images more appropriate

00:48:30.327 --> 00:48:32.127
"to Syria or Persia

00:48:32.127 --> 00:48:35.327
"than to any possible
American Indian society."

00:48:37.700 --> 00:48:41.410
At another level though,
these sparks of Islam

00:48:41.410 --> 00:48:42.790
in the historical record

00:48:42.790 --> 00:48:44.540
of the early Spanish colonial Caribbean

00:48:44.540 --> 00:48:48.100
offer us numerous ways of
conceiving of the place of Islam

00:48:48.100 --> 00:48:51.340
in the early modern world, and
allow us to see the history

00:48:51.340 --> 00:48:53.453
of the Americas in a new way.

00:48:54.708 --> 00:48:57.680
And these are gonna be my
closing, my closing thoughts

00:48:57.680 --> 00:48:59.253
on these two points.

00:49:00.790 --> 00:49:05.500
Columbus's biography with
Islam helps us to see that 1492

00:49:05.500 --> 00:49:09.060
was not the break it's
often portrayed to be.

00:49:09.060 --> 00:49:12.260
1492, the beginning of the modern world,

00:49:12.260 --> 00:49:16.040
1492 the end of the
independence sovereignty

00:49:17.109 --> 00:49:18.070
of the Americas.

00:49:18.070 --> 00:49:21.270
To properly and fully understand 1492,

00:49:21.270 --> 00:49:25.320
we must understand the continuity
that made and shaped it.

00:49:25.320 --> 00:49:27.750
Columbus's voyages across the Atlantic

00:49:28.703 --> 00:49:31.490
were a direct result of
one particular continuity

00:49:31.490 --> 00:49:35.200
that of Muslim Christian
animosity in the Mediterranean.

00:49:35.200 --> 00:49:38.690
The crucible of the crusades,
centuries of warfare,

00:49:38.690 --> 00:49:42.309
and the increasing encroachment
of the Ottomans west

00:49:42.309 --> 00:49:46.680
in the years after 1453 forged
in the minds of Columbus,

00:49:46.680 --> 00:49:50.620
Cortés, and thousands of other Europeans

00:49:50.620 --> 00:49:53.380
that their enemies were Muslims.

00:49:53.380 --> 00:49:55.820
That the notion of enemy to them

00:49:55.820 --> 00:49:57.793
had something to do with Islam.

00:49:58.640 --> 00:50:01.200
They took this long history
of warring with them

00:50:01.200 --> 00:50:03.790
across the Atlantic.

00:50:03.790 --> 00:50:05.520
Indeed, many of those
who ended up fighting

00:50:05.520 --> 00:50:08.790
the American Indians in the new world

00:50:08.790 --> 00:50:11.310
had fought Muslims in the old world.

00:50:11.310 --> 00:50:14.410
Some of them would even
come back from the Americas

00:50:14.410 --> 00:50:18.890
to fight again against
their Muslim enemies of old.

00:50:18.890 --> 00:50:22.360
Cortés, for example, waged
the last war of his life

00:50:22.360 --> 00:50:27.360
in Algiers in 1541 after
his exploits in Mexico.

00:50:28.820 --> 00:50:31.750
As one of the earliest 16th
century Spanish historians

00:50:31.750 --> 00:50:35.517
of the Americas wrote quote,
"The conquest of the Indians

00:50:35.517 --> 00:50:38.390
"began after that of
the Moors was completed

00:50:39.360 --> 00:50:42.110
"so that the Spaniards would
ever fights the infidels."

00:50:43.810 --> 00:50:46.980
Islam had pushed Europe's
crusades to the Caribbean

00:50:46.980 --> 00:50:49.270
where even on the other
side of the Atlantic

00:50:49.270 --> 00:50:52.500
Europeans still often
conceived of their enemies

00:50:52.500 --> 00:50:56.083
as somehow being related to Muslims.

00:50:57.070 --> 00:51:02.030
In narratives of 1492 as break,
Islam is always portrayed

00:51:02.030 --> 00:51:04.150
as being of the old world,

00:51:04.150 --> 00:51:06.310
part of all, that was left behind

00:51:07.427 --> 00:51:10.890
for a new European
beginning in a new world.

00:51:10.890 --> 00:51:14.150
It is precisely because
Islam was so central

00:51:14.150 --> 00:51:16.570
to the history of Europe in the old world

00:51:16.570 --> 00:51:20.220
that it had to be excised
from the histories of Europe

00:51:20.220 --> 00:51:21.083
in the new world.

00:51:21.990 --> 00:51:24.850
Historians have in many
ways followed the lead

00:51:24.850 --> 00:51:29.530
of many of these early Europeans
who went to the Americas.

00:51:29.530 --> 00:51:32.450
Those who sought to
relegate Islam to the past

00:51:32.450 --> 00:51:36.780
in favor of the future free
of this old world menace.

00:51:36.780 --> 00:51:40.070
Indeed beginning just a
few decades after Columbus

00:51:40.070 --> 00:51:42.320
and continuing for centuries after that,

00:51:42.320 --> 00:51:47.240
Europeans feared the transfer
of Islam to the Americas.

00:51:47.240 --> 00:51:49.810
Trying to keep Islam out of the new world

00:51:49.810 --> 00:51:53.570
meant also trying to keep it
out of many of the narratives

00:51:53.570 --> 00:51:57.790
of the making of the
modern world after 1492.

00:51:57.790 --> 00:52:00.250
If the Atlantic voyages are so crucial

00:52:00.250 --> 00:52:03.290
to the history of the
world after the year 1500,

00:52:03.290 --> 00:52:06.170
I think it's hard to argue
that they're not important.

00:52:06.170 --> 00:52:09.600
And if Islam is removed from the story,

00:52:09.600 --> 00:52:12.660
then Islam is never a
foundational additive

00:52:12.660 --> 00:52:15.453
or even relevant to this modern history.

00:52:16.445 --> 00:52:19.270
It stands apart, only
appearing every now and then

00:52:19.270 --> 00:52:22.590
as a violent enemy on the margins.

00:52:22.590 --> 00:52:26.610
Leaving out the story of Islam
in the shaping of Columbus,

00:52:26.610 --> 00:52:30.920
thus removes it from the early
history of the colonization

00:52:30.920 --> 00:52:32.283
of the Americas.

00:52:33.203 --> 00:52:36.340
It prevents us from seeing
the integral role Islam played

00:52:36.340 --> 00:52:39.280
in forming the violent early years

00:52:39.280 --> 00:52:41.543
of Spanish rule in the new world.

00:52:42.780 --> 00:52:46.090
However, if we accept that Islam

00:52:46.090 --> 00:52:50.004
is part of one of the
oldest cultural tropes

00:52:50.004 --> 00:52:53.460
in the new world, as long as the history

00:52:53.460 --> 00:52:57.670
of European colonialism and
disease in the Americas,

00:52:57.670 --> 00:53:02.530
if we accept that Islam was
the mold that cast the history

00:53:02.530 --> 00:53:06.060
of European racial and ethnic
thinking in the Americas,

00:53:06.060 --> 00:53:10.600
as well as the history of warfare
in the Western hemisphere,

00:53:10.600 --> 00:53:14.010
then we must admit that
Islam has to be a part

00:53:14.010 --> 00:53:17.520
of any understanding of
the history of the Americas

00:53:17.520 --> 00:53:18.720
from the very beginning.

00:53:19.770 --> 00:53:23.660
The idea of Islam as a deep
existential threat was there

00:53:23.660 --> 00:53:26.140
at the beginning of
Europeans stepping foot

00:53:26.140 --> 00:53:27.610
on these continents.

00:53:27.610 --> 00:53:31.210
The history of this idea
in the Western hemisphere

00:53:31.210 --> 00:53:33.730
is older than say Protestantism

00:53:33.730 --> 00:53:37.260
or any British or French
interests in the Americas.

00:53:37.260 --> 00:53:40.300
It's older than the
transatlantic slave trade.

00:53:40.300 --> 00:53:43.890
It's older than any nation
state in the Americas.

00:53:43.890 --> 00:53:46.500
It runs through all of
these histories though.

00:53:46.500 --> 00:53:50.100
And so beginning a history
of the Americas here

00:53:50.100 --> 00:53:53.800
tells very different stories
than those we're used to.

00:53:53.800 --> 00:53:57.140
Indeed, we might go so far as to say

00:53:57.140 --> 00:54:00.650
that the two origins of
European colonization

00:54:00.650 --> 00:54:03.800
in the Americas were it's crusading ethos

00:54:03.800 --> 00:54:05.800
of the kind that I've described today

00:54:05.800 --> 00:54:08.520
and of course the deadly confrontations

00:54:08.520 --> 00:54:12.110
with Native Americans, and
these are linked stories

00:54:12.110 --> 00:54:14.193
as I've tried to suggest today.

00:54:15.638 --> 00:54:19.190
Telling them together allows us to uncover

00:54:19.190 --> 00:54:21.100
a durable conceptual link

00:54:21.100 --> 00:54:23.850
in the European turned American psyche

00:54:23.850 --> 00:54:27.483
between violence against
Native Americans and Muslims.

00:54:28.360 --> 00:54:32.130
If the original language
of European warfare

00:54:32.130 --> 00:54:36.490
in this hemisphere derived
from Europe's Wars with Islam,

00:54:36.490 --> 00:54:40.601
from at least the 18th century
that language was formed

00:54:40.601 --> 00:54:43.970
by America's warfare
against native peoples.

00:54:43.970 --> 00:54:45.970
This is a historical reversal

00:54:45.970 --> 00:54:48.440
of profound importance that suggests

00:54:48.440 --> 00:54:51.130
that we should spend more time recognizing

00:54:52.020 --> 00:54:52.930
and thinking about the history

00:54:52.930 --> 00:54:57.210
of these seemingly disparate
yet bound cultures.

00:54:57.210 --> 00:55:00.650
Moreover, given that since
Vietnam, the primary theater

00:55:00.650 --> 00:55:03.600
of American war has been
in the Muslim world,

00:55:03.600 --> 00:55:06.790
with the attendant consequences
for Muslim Americans,

00:55:06.790 --> 00:55:10.720
it bears thinking deeply about
this long intertwined history

00:55:10.720 --> 00:55:14.053
of warfare, animosity and vilification.

00:55:15.660 --> 00:55:18.010
So this is admittedly a very long way

00:55:18.010 --> 00:55:19.910
from where we've been.

00:55:19.910 --> 00:55:22.490
I raise it though because
some of the strands

00:55:22.490 --> 00:55:24.540
of our present were spun

00:55:24.540 --> 00:55:28.683
by the story of Columbus and
Islam that I presented today.

00:55:29.575 --> 00:55:31.080
It's an obvious starting
point for the history

00:55:31.080 --> 00:55:35.920
of how European colonialism
affected the Americas.

00:55:35.920 --> 00:55:39.670
And I've tried to suggest that
it's also a starting point

00:55:39.670 --> 00:55:41.920
for thinking about how native peoples

00:55:41.920 --> 00:55:44.796
have been folded in to a long history

00:55:44.796 --> 00:55:48.062
of European Islamic relations.

00:55:48.062 --> 00:55:50.530
And this is a history I think
we need to assimilate better.

00:55:50.530 --> 00:55:54.720
Doing so would help to contribute
to the manifold efforts

00:55:54.720 --> 00:55:58.510
to understand histories of
European native encounters

00:55:58.510 --> 00:56:03.510
and the histories of European
polities in the Americas,

00:56:03.542 --> 00:56:05.040
and then eventually the
United States itself

00:56:05.040 --> 00:56:07.463
in a far more global scope.

00:56:08.954 --> 00:56:09.953
So I will end there.

00:56:09.953 --> 00:56:10.960
Thank you for your attention.

00:56:10.960 --> 00:56:14.933
And I really look forward to
your comments and questions.

00:56:14.933 --> 00:56:15.766
Thank you very much.

00:56:17.870 --> 00:56:18.703
- Thank you, Alan,

00:56:18.703 --> 00:56:22.733
for that wonderful talk
and contextualization.

00:56:23.720 --> 00:56:27.350
I think there are lots of
questions that we are getting

00:56:27.350 --> 00:56:32.350
on the chat, but I'm
burning to ask this question

00:56:34.090 --> 00:56:38.033
which is, you know, where do the Jewish,

00:56:44.396 --> 00:56:46.490
where does the Jewish expulsion

00:56:46.490 --> 00:56:49.010
and where do the Jewish peoples

00:56:49.010 --> 00:56:52.223
come in this mix of this encounter?

00:56:53.200 --> 00:56:57.840
Do you see them somehow as being a part

00:56:57.840 --> 00:57:00.003
or embroiled in this?

00:57:01.139 --> 00:57:04.460
And also what I'm really
curious is, you know,

00:57:04.460 --> 00:57:06.490
when I want to feel optimistic,

00:57:06.490 --> 00:57:11.490
it's hard at the current moment,
given the global alignment

00:57:12.340 --> 00:57:15.830
and the sort of animosities,
persistent animosities.

00:57:15.830 --> 00:57:19.836
I love how you've drawn,
sort of connected the dots

00:57:19.836 --> 00:57:22.890
and shown that these sort of animosity

00:57:24.372 --> 00:57:27.280
towards others has a very long history

00:57:27.280 --> 00:57:29.143
and a very deep history.

00:57:30.620 --> 00:57:34.072
So I was curious, you know,
if you think about Al-Andalus,

00:57:34.072 --> 00:57:36.420
right, there was this
moment, golden moment

00:57:36.420 --> 00:57:40.120
when these multiple cultures thrived.

00:57:40.120 --> 00:57:43.910
So do you, what do you think was key

00:57:43.910 --> 00:57:46.730
for that kind of thriving

00:57:46.730 --> 00:57:51.730
of these multiple religious
traditions and cultures?

00:57:54.500 --> 00:57:56.180
- Thank you for that.

00:57:56.180 --> 00:57:59.810
Yeah, so I didn't have time to go into

00:57:59.810 --> 00:58:03.220
the history of the Jews of Spain.

00:58:03.220 --> 00:58:06.100
It's a really important and
integral part to this story.

00:58:06.100 --> 00:58:08.293
I can answer it in a couple of ways.

00:58:09.900 --> 00:58:14.028
So one is, you know,
the major events of 1492

00:58:14.028 --> 00:58:17.450
related to Iberia are the
Reconquista, Columbus's voyages

00:58:17.450 --> 00:58:19.490
and the expulsion of the Jews.

00:58:19.490 --> 00:58:21.000
I talked about two of them.

00:58:21.000 --> 00:58:24.990
In, you know, there's an
Ottoman part to that story

00:58:24.990 --> 00:58:29.990
which is that most of Iberia's
Jews eventually end up in

00:58:31.480 --> 00:58:33.840
or more than any other single place,

00:58:33.840 --> 00:58:35.940
most of them end up in the Ottoman Empire.

00:58:37.160 --> 00:58:40.992
Eventually, this is after
some time in Portugal

00:58:40.992 --> 00:58:44.330
or in North Africa or in various
parts of the Mediterranean,

00:58:44.330 --> 00:58:47.300
but many eventually make their
way to the Ottoman Empire,

00:58:47.300 --> 00:58:50.130
and famously the city
of Salonica Thessaloniki

00:58:51.104 --> 00:58:53.728
in today's Greece becomes
the largest Jewish city

00:58:53.728 --> 00:58:54.750
in the world.

00:58:54.750 --> 00:58:57.206
And at various points
has a Jewish majority,

00:58:57.206 --> 00:58:59.208
so the first Jewish majority city--

00:58:59.208 --> 00:59:02.128
- Yes, many did go to
Italy as well, right?

00:59:02.128 --> 00:59:07.128
- Yes, yes, there are waves of
embarkations and expulsions.

00:59:08.910 --> 00:59:12.070
So sometimes people
settle in various places

00:59:12.070 --> 00:59:14.625
and then, and stay.

00:59:14.625 --> 00:59:16.040
Some people settle for a
little while and then move on.

00:59:16.040 --> 00:59:19.909
It's a very complicated, a
very complicated history,

00:59:19.909 --> 00:59:24.093
obviously, but in broad brush
strokes, that's part of the,

00:59:25.165 --> 00:59:27.891
that's part of the story
that I tell in my book.

00:59:27.891 --> 00:59:28.724
And that's part of the story here.

00:59:28.724 --> 00:59:32.803
There is a, and of course,
obviously anti-Judaism,

00:59:32.803 --> 00:59:37.470
anti-Semitism is rampant in
Spain before this period.

00:59:37.470 --> 00:59:41.109
Very famously the, you
know, there instances

00:59:41.109 --> 00:59:45.210
of waves of anti-Jewish
violence for centuries,

00:59:45.210 --> 00:59:48.003
but increasingly up to 1492.

00:59:49.140 --> 00:59:52.760
The sort of Catholic animosity
towards Jews and Muslims

00:59:52.760 --> 00:59:55.811
is quite different in its nature.

00:59:55.811 --> 01:00:00.811
In the case of Muslims,
it is more geopolitical

01:00:02.227 --> 01:00:04.433
and existential.

01:00:05.600 --> 01:00:08.840
In the case of Jews, it's more theological

01:00:08.840 --> 01:00:13.600
and at the level of notions

01:00:13.600 --> 01:00:18.600
of cleanliness, disease,
contagion in cities, right?

01:00:22.086 --> 01:00:26.230
And, you know, as I think
you were suggesting,

01:00:26.230 --> 01:00:30.060
I mean linking others
to disease is something

01:00:30.060 --> 01:00:31.530
that happens in Spain in this period

01:00:31.530 --> 01:00:32.993
as it is happening today.

01:00:34.050 --> 01:00:38.530
But it's because there's no equivalent

01:00:38.530 --> 01:00:41.051
of a Jewish Ottoman Empire.

01:00:41.051 --> 01:00:43.000
There is no external Jewish state

01:00:43.000 --> 01:00:47.960
that is a potential geopolitical
rival of a Catholic state.

01:00:47.960 --> 01:00:50.500
And so that makes the position of,

01:00:50.500 --> 01:00:53.329
and there are other reasons
obviously, of Jews and Muslims,

01:00:53.329 --> 01:00:54.612
very different in Spain.

01:00:54.612 --> 01:00:57.650
So this notion that the Muslims of Spain

01:00:57.650 --> 01:01:01.190
are a liable fifth column
for any Muslim polity,

01:01:01.190 --> 01:01:03.800
whether it be the Ottomans,
the Mamluks, et cetera,

01:01:03.800 --> 01:01:07.046
is one that doesn't exist for Jews.

01:01:07.046 --> 01:01:08.630
And so that gives it a very
different positionality

01:01:08.630 --> 01:01:11.563
of those communities within Spain.

01:01:12.960 --> 01:01:14.793
- Okay, thank you very much.

01:01:15.934 --> 01:01:19.732
So I think I'll handle a
couple of the questions,

01:01:19.732 --> 01:01:21.430
and Ty, then you can take over.

01:01:21.430 --> 01:01:26.103
So yeah, so there's a
question from Carlton Tucker,

01:01:27.116 --> 01:01:30.580
besides Marco Polo's writings
and crusades oral stories

01:01:30.580 --> 01:01:33.470
would Columbus have possibly
heard of the stories

01:01:33.470 --> 01:01:34.663
of Ibn Battuta?

01:01:36.151 --> 01:01:37.855
Some have called the Muslim,

01:01:37.855 --> 01:01:40.705
referred to him as the Muslim
Columbus a century earlier?

01:01:42.070 --> 01:01:44.374
- Yeah, well, the Muslim Marco Polo,

01:01:44.374 --> 01:01:46.203
not the Muslim Columbus, but yeah.

01:01:47.240 --> 01:01:49.860
I've seen no evidence
that Columbus knew of

01:01:49.860 --> 01:01:51.500
Ibn Battuta's writings.

01:01:51.500 --> 01:01:54.156
We know that he read various other tales.

01:01:54.156 --> 01:01:56.230
For example, the legend
of the seven cities

01:01:56.230 --> 01:02:00.970
of Cibola was very important for Columbus

01:02:00.970 --> 01:02:04.435
which is a story about, let's
see if I get this right,

01:02:04.435 --> 01:02:09.040
a group of bishops who flee Spain

01:02:09.040 --> 01:02:12.290
with Muslim ships that have a lot of gold.

01:02:12.290 --> 01:02:16.083
They settle on these mythic
islands somewhere in the sea.

01:02:16.940 --> 01:02:18.830
And so, you know, various explorers tried

01:02:18.830 --> 01:02:21.500
to these islands and the
gold that they possessed,

01:02:21.500 --> 01:02:24.820
again to fund a crusade
or for any other purpose,

01:02:24.820 --> 01:02:28.163
but I don't think Columbus
knew of Ibn Battuta.

01:02:30.880 --> 01:02:32.043
- Ty, did you want to?

01:02:33.014 --> 01:02:35.760
- Yes, sure, another question
submitted from a participant.

01:02:35.760 --> 01:02:40.436
A few years later Vasco da Gama
is said to have been guided

01:02:40.436 --> 01:02:44.920
from the east coast of Africa
to India by Arab sailors.

01:02:44.920 --> 01:02:47.920
He found Arab merchants
in India when he landed.

01:02:47.920 --> 01:02:50.410
How did he negotiate his relationship

01:02:50.410 --> 01:02:53.783
with these Moors in contrast to Columbus?

01:02:56.080 --> 01:02:59.440
- That's a good question that I don't,

01:02:59.440 --> 01:03:01.737
I don't think I have a, you know,

01:03:01.737 --> 01:03:04.670
a full answer to other
than to say, you know,

01:03:04.670 --> 01:03:09.670
Vasco da Gama shared in
a language of crusade

01:03:09.801 --> 01:03:11.943
as much as any of these other explorers.

01:03:13.110 --> 01:03:16.210
He doesn't, yeah,

01:03:16.210 --> 01:03:20.590
but that's what I would
answer to that question.

01:03:20.590 --> 01:03:23.895
I mean, you know,

01:03:23.895 --> 01:03:28.453
Columbus also uses various
Arab sailing technologies

01:03:29.630 --> 01:03:31.260
during his voyages.

01:03:31.260 --> 01:03:33.996
There's a complicated story
about the kind of sail

01:03:33.996 --> 01:03:37.170
that is used on the ships that
he takes across the Atlantic

01:03:37.170 --> 01:03:40.930
that combined the technology of the Dhow

01:03:40.930 --> 01:03:44.493
which is an Indian ocean
vessel that Vasco da Gama used.

01:03:45.570 --> 01:03:46.530
But, you know, in a general way,

01:03:46.530 --> 01:03:51.039
Vasco da Gama is steeped
in this same rhetoric,

01:03:51.039 --> 01:03:56.039
language and education of
crusade and is seeking, right,

01:03:57.120 --> 01:04:00.030
a way around the Muslim
empires of the Middle East

01:04:00.030 --> 01:04:01.780
as much as any of these others are.

01:04:04.450 --> 01:04:05.917
- There is a question,

01:04:05.917 --> 01:04:09.679
regarding Columbus's reasoning,

01:04:09.679 --> 01:04:11.700
doesn't the prevailing
current contradict the idea

01:04:11.700 --> 01:04:15.153
of wood detritus floating
East to West Africa?

01:04:17.180 --> 01:04:18.080
- Okay, I'm sorry.

01:04:18.921 --> 01:04:20.394
Could you repeat the question?

01:04:20.394 --> 01:04:23.300
- So the question is doesn't
the prevailing current

01:04:23.300 --> 01:04:26.420
contradict the idea of wood detritus

01:04:26.420 --> 01:04:29.677
floating from East to West Africa?

01:04:29.677 --> 01:04:30.682
- Oh, right, right.

01:04:30.682 --> 01:04:32.417
I see what you're saying.

01:04:32.417 --> 01:04:35.517
I guess it does, but it, yeah,

01:04:35.517 --> 01:04:36.922
I think the question
points to the fact that

01:04:36.922 --> 01:04:41.922
that notion of of wood
floating from across the ocean

01:04:42.410 --> 01:04:43.950
from Asia is wrong.

01:04:43.950 --> 01:04:47.000
That wood was probably
coming from the port itself

01:04:47.000 --> 01:04:50.163
or from somewhere further
South on the coast

01:04:50.163 --> 01:04:51.162
or something like that.

01:04:51.162 --> 01:04:52.520
Or maybe it wasn't even
worked by human hands at all,

01:04:52.520 --> 01:04:55.219
but when they looked at
it, they thought it was.

01:04:55.219 --> 01:04:56.403
Who knows?

01:04:56.403 --> 01:04:58.570
- Right, so then there's a question

01:04:58.570 --> 01:05:02.890
that is a bit tricky to
parse, but let me try.

01:05:02.890 --> 01:05:07.230
So how would you describe
your positionality

01:05:07.230 --> 01:05:10.963
or your lens in this research or book?

01:05:12.680 --> 01:05:15.223
I don't know if they want you to frame

01:05:15.223 --> 01:05:18.173
your political position,
your epistemic position.

01:05:19.025 --> 01:05:20.018
- I can answer that question.

01:05:20.018 --> 01:05:21.410
I'm happy to answer that question.

01:05:21.410 --> 01:05:23.473
So I would say in this talk,

01:05:24.964 --> 01:05:26.550
I really turned up the volume

01:05:26.550 --> 01:05:31.550
on an idea of civilizational conflict,

01:05:32.420 --> 01:05:35.100
one that historians

01:05:35.100 --> 01:05:37.263
have generally been
trying to get away from.

01:05:38.170 --> 01:05:41.233
And I admit that I wrestled with that,

01:05:42.338 --> 01:05:45.640
and I'll tell you a little
bit about why I decided

01:05:45.640 --> 01:05:48.323
to keep it, keep that language.

01:05:48.323 --> 01:05:50.510
One is that's the language of the sources.

01:05:50.510 --> 01:05:54.621
The sources, the Christian
sources and the Muslim sources,

01:05:54.621 --> 01:05:57.104
but, you know, focusing here
on Columbus and his writings

01:05:57.104 --> 01:05:58.098
and those of others,

01:05:58.098 --> 01:06:00.363
they use this language of
civilizational conflict,

01:06:00.363 --> 01:06:04.340
of millennial confrontation with Islam.

01:06:04.340 --> 01:06:07.170
Columbus spends the last
few years of his life

01:06:07.170 --> 01:06:12.170
writing a kind of compendia of ideas

01:06:12.440 --> 01:06:14.783
about the end times, right?

01:06:16.133 --> 01:06:21.133
He very much is steeped in
this, in this set of ideas

01:06:22.097 --> 01:06:27.097
around conquest warfare,
existential crisis of Christianity.

01:06:32.130 --> 01:06:35.175
So that's, I'm being true to the sources.

01:06:35.175 --> 01:06:39.163
The other sort of historiographical,
I want to say move,

01:06:40.825 --> 01:06:43.529
but challenge that I'm putting
forth is the following.

01:06:43.529 --> 01:06:45.752
So, you know, historians
of the Mediterranean

01:06:45.752 --> 01:06:50.752
and of other cross-cultural
zones, if you like,

01:06:50.976 --> 01:06:52.393
but the Mediterranean is the place

01:06:52.393 --> 01:06:53.914
where a lot of this work happens,

01:06:53.914 --> 01:06:55.870
have over the past few decades

01:06:55.870 --> 01:06:56.950
done a lot of really important work

01:06:56.950 --> 01:07:01.950
showing how confessional
boundaries might not have been

01:07:01.970 --> 01:07:04.030
as fixed as we think.

01:07:04.030 --> 01:07:06.800
Linguistic boundaries, you know,

01:07:06.800 --> 01:07:10.900
Italian traders were
living and marrying often

01:07:12.380 --> 01:07:13.720
in the Ottoman world.

01:07:13.720 --> 01:07:15.060
Muslims were in Europe.

01:07:15.060 --> 01:07:17.755
There's this sense of back and forth,

01:07:17.755 --> 01:07:22.755
almost a kind of happy go
lucky trading identities,

01:07:23.240 --> 01:07:26.263
religions, intermarriage,
language, et cetera.

01:07:27.560 --> 01:07:31.652
And that is obviously true to a point.

01:07:31.652 --> 01:07:34.480
I think we've overemphasized the idea that

01:07:35.420 --> 01:07:37.538
and there are all kinds of explanations

01:07:37.538 --> 01:07:38.715
for why we've done this,

01:07:38.715 --> 01:07:40.250
but we've overemphasized
the idea that something

01:07:40.250 --> 01:07:44.520
like religion might not matter
as much as we think it does.

01:07:44.520 --> 01:07:47.950
I think religion does matter
quite a lot to these people

01:07:47.950 --> 01:07:50.950
in very stark and important ways,

01:07:50.950 --> 01:07:55.950
geopolitically,
theologically, existentially.

01:07:56.460 --> 01:08:00.380
And so I wanna sort of re-insert that

01:08:00.380 --> 01:08:02.383
into the discussion here.

01:08:03.460 --> 01:08:06.750
You know, historians have,
and I've taught this work

01:08:06.750 --> 01:08:09.971
in my courses, have to great effect used

01:08:09.971 --> 01:08:12.730
the records of the inquisition.

01:08:12.730 --> 01:08:17.730
You know, obviously a
endeavor of religious violence

01:08:20.472 --> 01:08:23.840
to narrate other kinds
of histories, right,

01:08:23.840 --> 01:08:28.840
of commerce, of gender
relations, all kinds of things.

01:08:29.250 --> 01:08:30.500
And that's very, very useful again.

01:08:30.500 --> 01:08:34.070
But I think we have to understand

01:08:34.070 --> 01:08:36.270
the confessional structure

01:08:36.270 --> 01:08:38.170
in which much of this is taking place,

01:08:38.170 --> 01:08:41.691
and Columbus's relationship
with the Muslim world

01:08:41.691 --> 01:08:43.490
is one of the ways of doing that.

01:08:43.490 --> 01:08:45.630
But it does mean that, you know,

01:08:45.630 --> 01:08:47.520
in some ways for provocative purposes

01:08:47.520 --> 01:08:50.757
that I've turned up the volume more

01:08:50.757 --> 01:08:53.410
on the violent confrontation, warfare,

01:08:53.410 --> 01:08:54.675
civilizational crisis.

01:08:54.675 --> 01:08:56.340
- Yeah, but I'm curious because,

01:08:56.340 --> 01:08:58.050
so that's very interesting,

01:08:58.050 --> 01:09:02.518
but do you know that reframing
of the modern conflict

01:09:02.518 --> 01:09:06.378
is not particularly productive, right?

01:09:06.378 --> 01:09:08.456
So there's Ferguson and these other people

01:09:08.456 --> 01:09:10.856
who think, who are framing
the current situation

01:09:11.794 --> 01:09:13.400
as a clash of civilizations.

01:09:13.400 --> 01:09:16.252
Do you believe that is actually productive

01:09:16.252 --> 01:09:18.336
for the modern kind of, you know,

01:09:18.336 --> 01:09:20.217
mirroring today's struggle?

01:09:20.217 --> 01:09:21.354
- Yeah, yeah, no, no, no.

01:09:21.354 --> 01:09:23.267
I mean, and that, and
that's why we as historians

01:09:23.267 --> 01:09:24.994
have tended to move away from that.

01:09:24.994 --> 01:09:25.920
- [Priya] Right.

01:09:25.920 --> 01:09:28.500
- Nevertheless though, I
would push back a little bit

01:09:28.500 --> 01:09:31.500
to say, not to you, but to that work,

01:09:31.500 --> 01:09:35.383
to say that when you look at
the history of Christianity,

01:09:37.818 --> 01:09:42.150
there is an anti-Islamic
thread that runs through

01:09:42.150 --> 01:09:42.983
much of it.

01:09:43.977 --> 01:09:44.987
- [Priya] Right.

01:09:44.987 --> 01:09:45.989
- And it's pretty consistent.

01:09:45.989 --> 01:09:50.023
You know, let's say, you know
we can argue about when, and.

01:09:50.932 --> 01:09:52.115
- It runs deep.

01:09:52.115 --> 01:09:52.948
You're right, it runs very deep.

01:09:52.948 --> 01:09:55.070
- It ebbs and flows over time, et cetera.

01:09:55.070 --> 01:09:57.990
And I think, I think we
need to wrestle with that

01:09:57.990 --> 01:09:59.013
and take that seriously

01:09:59.013 --> 01:10:00.091
and understand why that is.

01:10:00.091 --> 01:10:01.960
Look, there's been a lot of
work done on antisemitism,

01:10:01.960 --> 01:10:03.790
the long history of antisemitism

01:10:03.790 --> 01:10:06.640
from antiquity, even
before Christianity, right?

01:10:06.640 --> 01:10:08.450
From antiquity to the present.

01:10:08.450 --> 01:10:10.853
You know, there's a whole set of books.

01:10:10.853 --> 01:10:12.473
There's been some on, you know,

01:10:12.473 --> 01:10:13.430
an equivalent sort of story about Islam

01:10:13.430 --> 01:10:16.450
in Christian thought, but I
think there's a lot more work

01:10:16.450 --> 01:10:17.703
that could be done there.

01:10:19.393 --> 01:10:21.040
So yes, I wrestled with
not wanting to tell

01:10:21.040 --> 01:10:23.100
the kind of clash of civilization stories

01:10:23.100 --> 01:10:25.506
that Muslims and Christians
have been fighting

01:10:25.506 --> 01:10:27.130
for all time, everywhere, always,

01:10:27.130 --> 01:10:30.240
and yet pretty consistently,

01:10:30.240 --> 01:10:32.780
at least from the period
that I know best, right,

01:10:32.780 --> 01:10:34.830
the kind of late medieval to the present,

01:10:35.740 --> 01:10:39.525
if you look at various
periods of Christian writing

01:10:39.525 --> 01:10:42.840
and thought, I'm talking about
European Christianity here,

01:10:42.840 --> 01:10:45.700
you do see that there is
a pretty consistent trend

01:10:45.700 --> 01:10:48.673
of anti-Muslim sentiments,

01:10:49.630 --> 01:10:53.423
writing about the theological
bankruptcy of Islam,

01:10:54.287 --> 01:10:59.287
talking about the political
corruption of Islam, et cetera.

01:11:00.527 --> 01:11:03.883
So that story needs to be told.

01:11:03.883 --> 01:11:04.716
I'm not doing that here,

01:11:04.716 --> 01:11:08.405
but that balance has to be kept in play

01:11:08.405 --> 01:11:11.283
of not doing the crude version
of clash of civilizations

01:11:11.283 --> 01:11:14.053
but nevertheless seeing
that there is a story here.

01:11:15.024 --> 01:11:16.980
- Right, so the next question

01:11:16.980 --> 01:11:21.605
is from a member of the audience.

01:11:21.605 --> 01:11:23.310
What I heard of Muslim
influence on Columbus

01:11:23.310 --> 01:11:26.650
is that Muslim sources had the best figure

01:11:26.650 --> 01:11:30.667
for the circumference of
the earth to find the Qibla,

01:11:31.892 --> 01:11:34.020
but they were expressed in Arab miles

01:11:34.020 --> 01:11:37.913
rather than the shorter
Roman miles used in Europe.

01:11:38.927 --> 01:11:40.080
Columbus used good figures,

01:11:40.080 --> 01:11:42.827
but with the wrong interpretation

01:11:42.827 --> 01:11:44.523
to argue that the voyage was shorter.

01:11:46.369 --> 01:11:49.040
- Yeah, I mean, I haven't gone deep in

01:11:49.040 --> 01:11:50.550
on that line on Columbus.

01:11:50.550 --> 01:11:52.600
Look, when you get into
the Columbia scholarship,

01:11:52.600 --> 01:11:55.224
it goes in all kinds of directions.

01:11:55.224 --> 01:11:56.870
- Right, and there's also issues of maps.

01:11:56.870 --> 01:11:59.750
I remember reading that
there were incorrect maps,

01:11:59.750 --> 01:12:01.163
that, yeah.

01:12:02.612 --> 01:12:04.500
So then the follow-up question is

01:12:04.500 --> 01:12:08.190
Genoa had colonies in the
Crimea as you pointed out,

01:12:08.190 --> 01:12:13.190
so I'm surprised that there
was such inaccurate information

01:12:13.340 --> 01:12:15.713
on the Mongols.

01:12:16.828 --> 01:12:18.428
There was no Great Khan by then.

01:12:20.190 --> 01:12:23.790
- Well, so the idea of the Great Khan,

01:12:23.790 --> 01:12:27.750
many historians think,
is based in some fact

01:12:29.200 --> 01:12:33.810
that being the Nestorian
Christians of Western China.

01:12:33.810 --> 01:12:37.860
So there is evidence that
during the Yuan period

01:12:37.860 --> 01:12:42.860
that there were people who
converted to Christianity

01:12:43.049 --> 01:12:47.360
and that that might have been one of,

01:12:47.360 --> 01:12:51.728
the reference for the
idea of the Great Khan

01:12:51.728 --> 01:12:53.968
having an interest in Christianity.

01:12:53.968 --> 01:12:56.610
- So there's an interesting question.

01:12:56.610 --> 01:12:59.524
Is there evidence that when
Cortés and others went back

01:12:59.524 --> 01:13:01.420
from the Americas that they took with them

01:13:01.420 --> 01:13:04.510
some of the people they
had conquered, and if so,

01:13:04.510 --> 01:13:06.760
were these individuals distributed

01:13:06.760 --> 01:13:09.323
by them in North Africa or in Europe?

01:13:10.988 --> 01:13:12.610
- You mean native peoples?

01:13:12.610 --> 01:13:14.245
- [Priya] Yeah, native peoples.

01:13:14.245 --> 01:13:17.090
- Yeah, yeah, no, native
peoples go back with Columbus

01:13:17.090 --> 01:13:20.480
and, you know, after that,
that's a quite common thing

01:13:20.480 --> 01:13:23.530
on various European expeditions

01:13:24.427 --> 01:13:26.943
for native peoples to go back to Europe,

01:13:27.870 --> 01:13:30.280
not back, but go to Europe

01:13:30.280 --> 01:13:32.460
and then sometimes back to the Americas,

01:13:32.460 --> 01:13:33.683
but sometimes not also.

01:13:35.420 --> 01:13:40.420
- Great, Ty, do you see
any further questions?

01:13:42.230 --> 01:13:43.229
- No, I don't.

01:13:43.229 --> 01:13:44.506
I think we got 'em all.

01:13:44.506 --> 01:13:45.511
- [Priya] We got them all.

01:13:45.511 --> 01:13:47.246
- [Deepti] Yeah, can I
just follow up my question,

01:13:47.246 --> 01:13:48.970
the Cortés question,

01:13:48.970 --> 01:13:51.409
about the native people?

01:13:51.409 --> 01:13:54.828
So were those people forced to assimilate

01:13:54.828 --> 01:13:57.433
into Christianity or Islam or?

01:13:58.580 --> 01:14:02.904
- Well, it depends what, you
know, who we're talking about,

01:14:02.904 --> 01:14:03.971
because--

01:14:03.971 --> 01:14:05.068
- [Deepti] No, since you mentioned

01:14:05.068 --> 01:14:07.413
that they were perceived as Muslim.

01:14:08.410 --> 01:14:10.187
- Right, no, no, no.

01:14:10.187 --> 01:14:12.560
I mean, so usually when
native peoples come,

01:14:12.560 --> 01:14:16.510
are brought to Europe,
they are sort of displayed

01:14:16.510 --> 01:14:21.510
as spectacles to be seen in cities,

01:14:23.487 --> 01:14:27.480
and any kind of religious conversion

01:14:27.480 --> 01:14:31.160
that they would have experienced

01:14:31.160 --> 01:14:35.650
or had forced upon them was
to Christianity not to Islam.

01:14:35.650 --> 01:14:37.512
- [Deepti] Okay, thank you.

01:14:37.512 --> 01:14:40.670
- So thank you so much, Alan,

01:14:40.670 --> 01:14:45.670
for this wonderful talk and
answering the vigorous Q &amp; A.

01:14:46.787 --> 01:14:50.100
And thanks everyone for attending

01:14:50.100 --> 01:14:51.850
and look forward to seeing you all

01:14:51.850 --> 01:14:53.153
at the next Franke event.

01:14:54.329 --> 01:14:55.327
- Thank you very much.

01:14:55.327 --> 01:14:56.160
- Thank you.

